hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1","The first thing I began mentioning thereof is what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 1 - [Book] on Purification. 1 - Chapter: water into which impurity falls. 1 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah ibn Rashid al-Basri narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Ubaydullah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Said al-Khudri: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform ablution from the well of Budaah. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, carcasses and menstrual cloths are thrown into it. He said: Verily water is not rendered impure."
"2","2 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud and Sulayman Abu Dawud al-Asadi narrated to us, both said: Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Salit ibn Ayyub, from Ubaydullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Rafi, from Abu Said al-Khudri, he said: It was said, O Messenger of Allah, water is drawn for you from the well of Budaah, and it is a well into which the excrement of people, the menstrual cloths of women, and the flesh of dogs are thrown. He said: Verily water is pure; nothing renders it impure."
"3","3 - Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Biraki narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim al-Qasmali narrated to us, he said: Mutarrif narrated to us, from Khalid ibn Abi Nawf, from Ibn Abi Said al-Khudri, from his father, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was performing ablution from the well of Budaah, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, do you perform ablution from it while stinking things that are thrown into it are cast therein? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Nothing renders water impure."
"4","4 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Hatim ibn Ismail narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abi Yahya al-Aslami, from his mother, she said: We visited Sahl ibn Sad along with four women, and he said: If I were to give you drink from the well of Budaah you would dislike that, yet I gave the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drink from it with my own hands."
"5","5 - Fahd ibn Sulayman ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said al-Asbahani said: Sharik ibn Abdillah al-Nakhai informed us, from Tarif al-Basri, from Abu Nadrah, from Jabir or Abu Said, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on our journey, and we came to a pond with a carcass in it, so we held back and the people held back until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us and said: What is the matter with you that you do not draw water? We said: O Messenger of Allah, this carcass. He said: Draw water, for verily nothing renders water impure. So we drew water and drank our fill. A group of people adopted these reports and said: Nothing that falls into water renders it impure unless it changes its color, taste, or smell; whichever of these occurs, the water has become impure. Others disagreed with them on that and said: As for what you mentioned about the well of Budaah, it is no proof for you, for there is disagreement about what the well of Budaah was. One group said: It was a channel of water to the gardens, so the water did not settle in it, and the ruling of its water was like the ruling of river water. And we say the same about every place of this description into whose water impurity falls: its water is not rendered impure unless it overcomes its taste, color, or smell, or it is known that the impurity is in the water taken from it; if that is known it is impure, and if it is not known it is pure. 6 - And this view we mentioned about the well of Budaah has been reported from al-Waqidi. Abu Jafar Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated it to me, from Abu Abdillah Muhammad ibn Shuja al-Thalji, from al-Waqidi: that it was so. And among the proof for that is also that they have agreed that when impurity falls into a well and overcomes the taste, smell, or color of its water, its water has become spoiled. And there is nothing of this in the hadith of the well of Budaah; rather in it is only that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the well of Budaah and it was said to him: Dogs and menstrual cloths are thrown into it, so he said: Verily nothing renders water impure. And we know that a well, if less than that fell into it, it would be impossible for the smell and taste of its water not to change; this is of what is reasoned and known. Since that is so, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted them its water, and they agreed that change from any of the aspects we mentioned had not affected the water, it is impossible according to us - and Allah knows best - that their asking the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about its water and his answering them with what he answered was while the impurity was in the well. Rather it - and Allah knows best - was after the impurity had been removed from the well, so they asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that: does it become pure by removing the impurity from it, so its water that comes upon it after that is not impure? And that is a problematic point, because the walls of the well were not washed and its mud did not come out, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: Verily water is not impure, meaning thereby the water that came upon it after the removal of the impurity from it, not that water is not rendered impure when impurity mixes with it. And we have seen him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The believer is not rendered impure."
"6","7 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated it to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Adi narrated to us, from Humayd, (H) 8 - and Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Humayd, from Bakr, from Abu Rafi, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: I met the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while I was in a state of major impurity, so he extended his hand to me and I withheld my hands from him and said: I am in a state of major impurity. He said: Glory be to Allah, verily the Muslim is not rendered impure. And he (peace be upon him) said in another hadith: Verily the earth is not rendered impure."
"7","9 - Abu Bakrah Bakkar ibn Qutaybah al-Bakrawi narrated that to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Aqil al-Dawraqi narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan narrated that when the delegation of Thaqif came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he pitched a tent for them in the mosque, and they said: O Messenger of Allah, an impure people. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is nothing of the impurities of people upon the earth; the impurities of people are only upon themselves. So the meaning of his saying: The Muslim is not rendered impure, was not intended thereby that his body is not rendered impure even if impurity touches it; rather he intended that he is not rendered impure in another sense. Likewise his saying: The earth is not rendered impure, he did not mean thereby that it is not rendered impure even if impurity touches it. And how could that be, when he ordered that the spot in the mosque where the Bedouin urinated have a bucket of water poured over it?"
"8","10 - Abu Bakrah narrated that to us, he said: Umar ibn Yunus al-Yamami narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Talhah narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Malik narrated to me, he said: While we were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a Bedouin came and stood urinating in the mosque. The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Stop, stop. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave him. So they left him until he urinated. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called him and said to him: Verily these mosques are not fit for any of this urine and filth; they are only for the remembrance of Allah, prayer, and recitation of the Quran. Ikrimah said: Or as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said. Then he ordered a man, who brought a bucket of water and poured it over it."
"9","11 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Said, that he heard Anas ibn Malik narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) something similar, except that he did not mention his saying: Verily these mosques, to the end of the hadith."
"10","And Tawus reported that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered that its place be dug out. 12 - Abu Bakrah Bakkar ibn Qutaybah al-Bakrawi narrated that to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Tawus, to that effect. And it has also been reported from Abdillah ibn Masud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), to that effect."
"11","13 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Saman ibn Malik al-Asadi, from Abu Wail, from Abdillah, he said: A Bedouin urinated in the mosque, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered regarding him, and a bucket of water was poured over it, then he ordered regarding it and its place was dug out. Abu Jafar said: So the meaning of his saying: Verily the earth is not rendered impure, is that it does not remain impure when the impurity is removed from it, not that he intended that it is not impure while the impurity is in it. Likewise his saying about the well of Budaah: Verily nothing renders water impure, is not about the state when the impurity is in it; rather it is about the state when there is no impurity in it. This is the meaning of his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the well of Budaah: Water is not rendered impure by anything - and Allah knows best - and we have seen him clarify that in another hadith."
"12","14 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn al-Harith al-Ansari and Ali ibn Shaybah ibn al-Salt al-Baghdadi narrated to us, both said: Abdillah ibn Yazid al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn Awn narrate, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, that he said: It was forbidden, or he was forbidden, that a man urinate in standing water - or stagnant water - then perform ablution or bathe from it."
"13","15 - And Ali ibn Mabad ibn Nuh al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: None of you should urinate in standing water that does not flow and then bathe in it."
"14","16 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala Abu Musa al-Sadafi narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad al-Laythi informed me, from al-Harith ibn Abi Dhubab, who is a man of Azd, from Ata ibn Mina, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None of you should urinate in standing water and then perform ablution from it or drink."
"15","17 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Bukayr ibn Abdillah ibn al-Ashajj narrated to him that Abu al-Saib, the freedman of Hisham ibn Zuhrah, narrated to him that he heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: None of you should bathe in standing water while he is in a state of major impurity. He said: How should he do, O Abu Hurayrah? He said: He should take from it by scooping."
"16","18 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Said ibn al-Hakam ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad informed us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Musa ibn Abi Uthman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: None of you should urinate in standing water that does not flow, then bathe from it. And similarly."
"17","19 - Husayn ibn Nasr ibn al-Muarik al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, may Allah have mercy on him. And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad. Then he mentioned something similar with its chain."
"18","20 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Lahiah narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman al-Araj narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: None of you should urinate in standing water that does not flow, then bathe from it."
"19","21 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Zuraah Wahbullah ibn Rashid narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh informed us, he said: I heard Ibn Ajlan narrate, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: None of you should urinate in stagnant water, nor bathe in it."
"20","22 - Ibrahim ibn Munqidh al-Usfuri narrated to us, he said: Idris ibn Yahya narrated to me, he said: Abdillah ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), something similar, except that he said: And no one in a state of major impurity should bathe in it."
"21","23 - And Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj ibn Sulayman al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abu Yusuf narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he forbade urinating in stagnant water and then performing ablution in it. Abu Jafar said: Since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) specified stagnant water that does not flow, to the exclusion of flowing water, we learn thereby that he made that distinction only because impurity permeates water that does not flow, and does not permeate flowing water. And there has also been reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning washing the vessel from the lapping of a dog that which we shall mention in another place of this book of ours, Allah the Exalted willing. That is proof of the impurity of the vessel and the impurity of its water, though it does not overcome its smell, color, or taste. So reconciling the meanings of these reports requires, regarding what we mentioned in this chapter of the meaning of the hadith of the well of Budaah, what we have described, so that its meanings agree with the meanings of these reports and do not contradict them. This is the ruling of water that does not flow when impurity falls into it, by way of reconciling the meanings of the reports. Except that a group of people fixed a measure in that and said: When water amounts to two qullahs, it does not carry filth. And they argued for that with that which [follows]."
"22","24 - Bahr ibn Nasr ibn Sabiq al-Khawlani narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Abu Usamah Hammad ibn Usamah narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Kathir al-Makhzumi, from Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn al-Zubayr, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdillah, from Abdillah ibn Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about water and the beasts of prey that come to it, and he said: When water reaches two qullahs, it does not carry filth. And similarly."
"23","25 - al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, I heard Yazid ibn Harun say: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn al-Zubayr, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdillah ibn Umar, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he was asked about the ponds in the desert from which beasts of prey drink, and he said: When water reaches two qullahs, it does not carry filth."
"24","26 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, Abbad ibn Abbad al-Muhallabi narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Jafar, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdillah ibn Umar, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), something similar. And similarly."
"25","27 - Yazid ibn Sinan ibn Yazid al-Basri narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Jafar, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdillah ibn Umar, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), something similar."
"26","28 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us that Asim ibn al-Mundhir informed them, he said: We were in a garden of ours - or a garden of Ubaydullah ibn Abdillah ibn Umar - and the time of prayer came - the noon prayer - so he went to the well of the garden and performed ablution from it, and in it was the skin of a dead camel. I said: Do you perform ablution from it while this is in it? Ubaydullah said: My father informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When water is two qullahs, it is not rendered impure. - And similarly."
"27","29 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, then he mentioned something similar with its chain, except that he did not raise it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), but stopped it at Ibn Umar. These people said: When water reaches this amount, whatever impurity falls into it does not harm it, except what overcomes its smell, taste, or color. And they argued for that with this hadith of Ibn Umar. So part of the proof against them, for the adherents of the view we have upheld, is that these two qullahs - their measure has not been explained to us in these reports. It is possible that their measure is two qullahs of the jars of Hajar, as you mentioned, and it is also possible that they are two qullahs intended thereby to be the two qullahs of a man, which is his stature; so what is meant is: when water is two qullahs - i.e. two statures - it does not carry filth due to its abundance and because it thereby has the status of rivers. If you say: The report according to us is upon its apparent meaning, and the jars are the well-known jars of Hijaz. It is said to you: If the report is upon its apparent meaning as you mentioned, then water that reaches that measure should not be harmed by impurity even if it changes its color, taste, or smell, because the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not mention that in this hadith, so the hadith is upon its apparent meaning. If you say: Even if he did not mention it in this hadith, he mentioned it in another, then you mentioned that which [follows]."
"28","30 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from al-Ahwas ibn Hakim, from Rashid ibn Sad, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Nothing renders water impure except what overcomes its color, taste, or smell. It is said to you: This is disconnected, and you do not affirm the disconnected nor argue with it. If you have taken his saying about the two qullahs upon a specific kind of jars, it is permissible for others to take water upon a specific kind of waters, so that according to him it accords with the meanings of the first reports and does not contradict them. Since the first reports that came about urinating in stagnant water and about the impurity of water in a vessel from the lapping of a cat therein are general, with no measure mentioned, and are applied to all water that does not flow, it is established thereby that what is in the hadith of the two qullahs is about flowing water, and no regard is given therein to the measure of the water, just as no regard was given to the measure in anything we mentioned, so that none of the reports narrated in this chapter contradict one another. And this meaning upon which we have upheld the meanings of these reports is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. And there has been reported in that from those before them that which agrees with their school. And among what was reported in that is that which [follows]."
"29","31 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mansur narrated to us, from Ata, that an Abyssinian fell into Zamzam and died, so Ibn al-Zubayr ordered and its water was drawn out, and the water kept flowing uninterruptedly. Then he looked and there was a spring flowing from the side of the Black Stone, so Ibn al-Zubayr said: That is enough for you. And what [follows]:"
"30","32 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us. al-Firyabi narrated to us. Sufyan narrated to us, Jabir informed me, from Abu al-Tufayl, he said: A boy fell into Zamzam, so it was emptied, i.e. its water was drawn out. And what [follows]:"
"31","33 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from Maysarah, that Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said about a well into which a mouse fell and died. He said: Its water is drawn out. And what [follows]:"
"32","34 - Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ruayni narrated to us. He said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us. He said: Musa ibn Ayan narrated to us, from Ata, from Maysarah and Zadha, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When a mouse or an animal falls into a well, draw from it until the water overcomes you."
"33","35 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Abu al-Muhazzam, he said: We asked Abu Hurayrah about a man who passes by a pond: should he urinate in it? He said: No, for his Muslim brother passes by it and drinks from it and performs ablution; but if it is flowing, let him urinate in it if he wishes. And what [follows]:"
"34","36 - Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us. He said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayrah, something similar. And what [follows]:"
"35","37 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Zakariyya, from al-Shabi, about a bird, a cat, and the like falling into a well. He said: Forty buckets are drawn from it."
"36","38 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us. He said: al-Firyabi narrated to us. Sufyan narrated to us, from Zakariyya, from al-Shabi, he said: Forty buckets are drawn from it. And what [follows]:"
"37","39 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Sabrah al-Hamdani, from al-Shabi, he said: Draw seventy buckets from it. And what [follows]:"
"38","40 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated it to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Hafs ibn Ghiyath al-Nakhai narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Sabrah al-Hamdani, from al-Shabi, he said: We asked him about a chicken that falls into a well and dies in it? He said: Seventy buckets are drawn from it. And what [follows]:"
"39","41 - Salih said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah informed us, from Ibrahim, about a well into which a large rat or a cat falls and dies? He said: Draw forty buckets from it. Mughirah said: Until the water changes. And what [follows]:"
"40","42 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim, about a mouse that fell into a well. He said: About forty buckets are drawn from it. And what [follows]:"
"41","43 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us. He said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim, about a well into which a mouse falls. He said: Buckets are drawn from it. And what [follows]:"
"42","44 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Abi Sulayman, that he said about a chicken that fell into a well and died: About forty or fifty buckets are drawn from it, then ablution is performed from it. So these whom we have reported from, among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and their followers, deemed the waters of wells impure by impurities falling into them, and did not consider their abundance or scarcity, but considered their stillness and stagnation, and distinguished between them and flowing water besides them. To these reports, along with what preceded them of what we reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), our companions adhered regarding impurities that fall into wells, and it was not permissible for them to contradict them because nothing contrary was reported from anyone. If a sayer says: You have deemed the water of a well impure by impurity falling into it, so that well should never become pure ever, because its walls have absorbed that impure water and it has settled in them, so it should be filled in. It is said to him: Customs are not seen to have run upon this. Abdillah ibn al-Zubayr did what we mentioned regarding Zamzam in the presence of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they did not object to him, nor did those after them object, nor did any of them consider filling it in. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered regarding a vessel rendered impure by the lapping of a dog in it that it be washed, and did not order that it be broken, though it had absorbed the impure water. Just as breaking that vessel was not ordered, likewise filling in that well is not ordered. If a sayer says: We have seen a vessel is washed, so why was the well not likewise? It is said to him: A well cannot be washed, because what it is washed with returns into it, unlike a vessel from which what it is washed with is poured out. Since a well is of what cannot be washed, and its purity in some state has been established, and everyone who obliged its impurity by impurity falling into it has obliged its purity by drawing from it, even if the mud in it is not drawn out - since the remaining of its mud in it does not necessitate the impurity of the water that newly comes into it even if it runs over that mud - then what is between its walls is even more deserving of not being impure. Had that been taken by way of reasoning, it would not have become pure until its walls were washed and its mud removed and it was dug out. Since they agreed that drawing out its mud and digging it out is not obligatory, washing its walls is even more deserving of not being obligatory. And all of this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"43","2 - Chapter: leftover of the cat. 45 - Yunus ibn Abd al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Wahb informed us, that Malik narrated to him, from Ishaq ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Talhah, from Humaydah bint Ubayd ibn Rifaah, from Kabshah bint Kab ibn Malik - and she was under Ibn Abi Qatadah - that Abu Qatadah entered upon her and she poured ablution water for him. A cat came and drank from it, so Abu Qatadah tilted the vessel for it until it drank. Kabshah said: He saw me looking at him, so he said: Do you wonder, O daughter of my brother? She said: I said: Yes. He said: Verily the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Verily she is not impure; she is of those who go around you, male or female."
"44","46 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, from Kab ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his grandfather Abu Qatadah, he said: I saw him performing ablution, then a cat came and he tilted it for it until it drank from the vessel. I said: O my father, why do you do this? He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do it, or he said: She is of those who go around you."
"45","47 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Rijal narrated to us, from his mother Amrah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I used to bathe, I and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from one vessel, though a cat had gotten to it before that."
"46","48 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Harithah ibn Abi al-Rijal, may Allah have mercy on him. 49 - And Abu Bishr Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Harithah ibn Muhammad, from Amrah, from Aishah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), something similar."
"47","50 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Amr al-Khurasani narrated to us, he said: Salih ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Aishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to tilt the vessel for the cat and perform ablution with its leftover. Abu Jafar said: A group of people adopted these reports and saw no harm in the leftover of the cat, and among those who held that are Abu Yusuf and Muhammad. Others disagreed with them and disliked it. Part of the proof of the latter against the adherents of the first view is that the hadith of Malik from Ishaq ibn Abdillah is no proof for you in the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that she is not impure, she is of those who go around you, male or female. For that may have been intended to mean her presence in houses and her touching clothes. As for her lapping in a vessel, there is no proof therein whether that necessitates impurity or not. What is in the hadith of that is only the act of Abu Qatadah. So it is not proper to argue from the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with what may bear the meaning argued with therein and may bear its contrary. We have seen that the presence of dogs in dwellings is not disliked, while their leftover is disliked. So it is also possible that what was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Abu Qatadah was intended to mean presence in dwellings for hunting, guarding, and crops. There is no proof therein about the ruling of their leftover, whether it is disliked or not. But the other reports from Aishah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), contain permission of her leftover. So we want to see whether anything contrary to them was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"48","51 - So we looked into that, and Abu Bakrah had narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Qurrah ibn Khalid, he said: Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The purification of a vessel when a cat licks in it is that it be washed once or twice; Qurrah doubted. This is a hadith with a connected chain, containing what contradicts the first reports, and this hadith has decisively distinguished them due to the soundness of its chain. If this matter is taken from the angle of the chain, then the saying with this is worthier than the saying with what contradicts it."
"49","52 - If a sayer says: Hisham ibn Hassan has narrated this hadith from Muhammad ibn Sirin and did not raise it, and he mentioned in that what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The leftover of the cat is poured out and the vessel is washed once or twice. It is said to him: There is nothing in this that necessitates the corruption of the hadith of Qurrah, for Muhammad ibn Sirin used to do this in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah, stopping them at him; when he was asked about them: Are they from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He would raise them. 53 - And the proof of that is what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abdillah al-Harawi narrated to us. He said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Atiq, from Muhammad ibn Sirin: that when he narrated from Abu Hurayrah and was asked, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Every hadith of Abu Hurayrah is from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He only did that because Abu Hurayrah would narrate to them only from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so what he had informed them of that sufficed, in the hadith of Ibn Abi Dawud, from raising every hadith Muhammad narrated to them from him. Thereby the connectedness of this hadith of Abu Hurayrah is established, along with the firmness, precision, and mastery of Qurrah. Then that has also been reported from Abu Hurayrah as stopped, from another path, but it is not raised."
"50","54 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Abu Salih al-Samman, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: A vessel is washed from the cat as it is washed from the dog."
"51","55 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, from Khayr ibn Nuaym, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, something similar. And that has been reported from a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and their followers."
"52","56 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Nafi, the freedman of Ibn Umar, narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Umar, that he would not perform ablution with the leftover of a dog or a cat. Other than that, there is no harm in it."
"53","57 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Rabi ibn Yahya al-Ushnani narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Waqid ibn Muhammad, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that he said: Do not perform ablution from the leftover of a donkey, a dog, or a cat."
"54","58 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Abi Abdillah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Said, he said: When a cat licks in a vessel, wash it twice and thrice."
"55","59 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us. He said: Hammad narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan and Said ibn al-Musayyab, about a cat licking in a vessel. He said: One of them would wash it once, and the other said: wash it twice."
"56","60 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb ibn Sulayman al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Qatadah, he said: Said ibn al-Musayyab and al-Hasan used to say: Wash the vessel thrice, meaning from the leftover of the cat."
"57","61 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Hurrah narrated to us, from al-Hasan, about a cat that licked in a vessel or drank from it. He said: It is poured out and the vessel is washed once."
"58","62 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj al-Qattan narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me that he asked Yahya ibn Said about the animals whose leftover is not used for ablution. He said: The pig, the dog, and the cat. This saying has been strengthened by sound reasoning, and that is that we have seen meats to be of four kinds: (1) pure eaten meat, which is the meat of camels, cattle, and sheep, so the leftover of all that is pure, because it touched pure meat. (2) Pure uneaten meat, which is the meat of the children of Adam, and their leftover is pure, because it touched pure meat. (3) Forbidden meat, which is the meat of the pig and the dog, so the leftover of that is forbidden, because it touched forbidden meat. So the ruling of what touched these three meats is as we mentioned; its ruling is their ruling in purity and prohibition. (4) Also among meats is meat forbidden to eat, which is the meat of domestic donkeys and every fanged beast of prey. Among that is the cat and what resembles it, so that was prohibited, forbidden to eat its meat by the Sunnah. And by reasoning also, the leftover of that has the ruling of its meat, because it touched disliked meat, so its ruling became its ruling, just as the ruling of what touched the first three meats became their ruling. Thereby the dislike of the leftover of the cat is established. With this we take, and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, may Allah have mercy on him."
"59","3 - Chapter: leftover of the dog. 63 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, from Shubah, from al-Amash, from Dhakwan, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When a dog licks in a vessel, wash it seven times."
"60","64 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, he said: al-Amash narrated to us, he said: Abu Salih narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), something similar."
"61","65 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), something similar, and he added: the first of them with dust."
"62","66 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Qurrah, he said: Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), something similar."
"63","67 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: Said was asked about a dog licking in a vessel, so he informed us, from Qatadah, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), something similar, except that he said: the first of them or the seventh with dust - Said doubted. A group of people adopted this report and said: A vessel is not purified when a dog licks in it until it is washed seven times, the first of them with dust, as the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said. Others disagreed with them and said: The vessel is washed from that as it is washed from other impurities, and they argued for that with what has been reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"64","68 - Among that is what Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, may Allah have mercy on him. And Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, he said: Said ibn al-Musayyab narrated to us that Abu Hurayrah used to say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you rises at night, let him not put his hand into the vessel until he pours over it twice or thrice, for none of you knows where his hand spent the night."
"65","69 - Ibn Abi Dawud and Fahd narrated to us, both said: Abu Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to me, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn Musafir narrated to me, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from Said and Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), something similar."
"66","70 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Zaidah ibn Qudamah informed us, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), something similar."
"67","71 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Abdillah ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Salih and Abu Razin, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), something similar, except that he said: Let him wash his hands twice or thrice."
"68","72 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, and from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), something similar."
"69","73 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Jabir ibn Ismail, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he rose from sleep, would pour over his hands thrice. They said: Since this was reported from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about purification from urine, because they used to relieve themselves and urinate and would not clean with water, so he ordered them with that when they rose from their sleep, because they do not know where their hands spent the night on their bodies, and it is possible that they were in a place they had wiped of urine or excrement, then they sweat, so their hands become impure thereby. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered them to wash them thrice, and that was their purification from excrement or urine if it had touched them. Since that purifies from urine and excrement, which are the gravest impurities, it is worthier that what is lesser than that among impurities be purified thereby. And what was reported from Abu Hurayrah of his saying after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has indicated what we mentioned of this, as [follows]:"
"70","74 - Ismail ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam ibn Harb narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik, from Ata, from Abu Hurayrah, about a vessel in which a dog or a cat licks. He said: It is washed three times. Since Abu Hurayrah held that three washings purify a vessel from the lapping of a dog in it, and what we mentioned has been reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), thereby the abrogation of seven is established, for we think well of him and do not imagine of him that he would abandon what he heard from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except to something similar; otherwise his probity would fall and his saying and narration would not be accepted. Had it been obligatory to act upon what we reported about seven and not deem it abrogated, then what Abdillah ibn al-Mughaffal reported in that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would be worthier than what Abu Hurayrah reported, because he added over him."
"71","75 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir and Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, both said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu al-Tayyah, from Mutarrif ibn Abdillah, from Abdillah ibn al-Mughaffal, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the killing of dogs, then said: What have I to do with dogs. Then he said: When a dog licks in the vessel of one of you, let him wash it seven times, and rub the eighth with dust."
"72","76 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from Shubah; then he mentioned something similar. So this Abdillah ibn al-Mughaffal has reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that it is washed seven times and the eighth is rubbed with dust, and he added over Abu Hurayrah, and the one who adds is worthier than the deficient. So this opponent of ours should have said: A vessel is not purified until it is washed eight times, the seventh with dust and the eighth likewise, to take both hadiths together. If he abandons the hadith of Abdillah ibn al-Mughaffal, then what his adversary obliged him in abandoning the seven we mentioned has bound him; otherwise we have explained that the gravest impurities are purified from by washing a vessel three times, so what is lesser than them is worthier to be purified by that also. And al-Hasan has said in that with what Abdillah ibn al-Mughaffal reported."
"73","77 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Hurrah narrated to us, from al-Hasan, he said: When a dog licks in a vessel, it is washed seven times and the eighth with dust. As for reasoning in that, what we explained of the ruling of meats in the chapter of the leftover of the cat has sufficed us the discussion therein. A group of people held about a dog licking in a vessel that the water is pure and the vessel is washed seven times, and they said: That is only devotion, by which we are devoted in vessels specifically. Part of the proof against them is that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the ponds to which beasts of prey come, he said: When water is two qullahs, it does not carry filth. That has indicated that when it is less than two qullahs, it carries filth; had it not been so, there would be no meaning to mentioning two qullahs, and what is less than them and what is more would be equal. Since mention ran upon two qullahs, it is established that their ruling is contrary to the ruling of what is less than them. Thereby it is established from the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that the lapping of a dog in water renders the water impure. And all we explained in this chapter is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them."
"74","4 - Chapter: leftover of the children of Adam. 78 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Mualla ibn Asad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Abdillah ibn Sarjis, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a man to bathe with the leftover of a woman, and a woman with the leftover of a man, but let them both draw together."
"75","79 - Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us; he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abdillah al-Awdi, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: I met one who accompanied the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as Abu Hurayrah accompanied him four years. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade, then he mentioned something similar."
"76","80 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, from Shubah, from Asim al-Ahwal, he said: I heard Abu Hajib narrate from al-Hakam al-Ghifari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade a man to perform ablution with the leftover of a woman or with the leftover of a woman - Abu Hajib does not know which of the two he said."
"77","81 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Sawadah ibn Asim Abu Hajib, from al-Hakam al-Ghifari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the leftover of a woman. Abu Jafar said: A group of people adopted these reports and disliked that a man perform ablution with the leftover of a woman or a woman perform ablution with the leftover of a man. Others disagreed with them and said: There is no harm in all of this. And among what they argued with in that is that which [follows]."
"78","81a - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, from Shubah, from Asim, from Muadhah, the wife of Silah ibn Ashyam, from Aishah, she said: I and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to bathe from one vessel."
"79","82 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Asim. Then he mentioned something similar with its chain."
"80","83 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Shihab narrated to me, from Urwah, from Aishah, something similar."
"81","84 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, something similar."
"82","85 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Hafs, from Urwah, from Aishah, something similar."
"83","86 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yala ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from Hurayth, from al-Shabi, from Masruq, from Aishah, something similar."
"84","87 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Mansur ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his mother, from Aishah, something similar."
"85","88 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Shayban narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman informed me, from Zaynab bint Umm Salamah, from Umm Salamah, she said: I used to bathe, I and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from one vessel."
"86","89 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir ibn Zayd, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Maymunah informed me that she used to bathe, she and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from one vessel."
"87","90 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from al-Hakam ibn Utaybah, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, she said: I used to bathe, I and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from one vessel."
"88","91 - Yazid ibn Sinan al-Basri narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Rabah ibn Abi Maruf narrated to us, from Ata, from Aishah, something similar."
"89","92 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Nuaym ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Yazid informed us, he said: I heard Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz al-Araj say: Naim, the freedman of Umm Salamah, narrated to me, from Umm Salamah, she said: I used to bathe, I and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from one tub, pouring over our hands until we cleaned them, then pouring water over ourselves."
"90","93 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us; he said: Shubah informed us, may Allah have mercy on him. 94 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Abdillah ibn Jabir, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to bathe, he and a woman of his wives, from one vessel. Abu Jafar said: According to us, this contains no proof against what the adherents of the first view say, for it is possible that they both bathed together."
"91","95 - Rather the dispute among people is when one of them begins before the other. So we looked into that, and Ali ibn Mabad had narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from Salim, from Umm Subayyah al-Juhaniyyah, he said: And he claimed that she had met and pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). She said: My hand and the hand of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) differed in ablution from one vessel."
"92","96 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Usamah informed me, from Salim ibn al-Numan, from Umm Subayyah al-Juhaniyyah, something similar. So in this is proof that one of them would take from the water after his companion."
"93","97 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Samaah narrated to us, from Ikrimah, from Aishah, she said: I used to bathe, I and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from one vessel, he beginning before me. So in this is proof that the leftover of a man is permissible for a woman to purify with."
"94","98 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Aflah ibn Humayd, from al-Qasim, from Aishah, she said: I used to bathe, I and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from one vessel, our hands differing in it, from major impurity."
"95","99 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Maslamah ibn Qanab narrated to us, he said: Aflah narrated to us, may Allah have mercy on him."
"96","100 - And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Aflah narrated to us, then both mentioned something similar with its chain."
"97","101 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Sufyan informed us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, she said: I used to contend, I and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in bathing from one vessel, from major impurity."
"98","102 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Aishah, that she and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to bathe from one vessel, he scooping before her and she scooping before him."
"99","103 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Mubarak ibn Fadalah, from his mother, from Muadhah, from Aishah, she said: I used to bathe, I and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from one vessel, and I would say: Leave for me, leave for me."
"100","104 - Muhammad ibn al-Abbas ibn al-Rabi al-Lului narrated to us, he said: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: al-Mubarak narrated to us, then he mentioned something similar with its chain."
"101","105 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Yazid al-Rishk, from Muadhah, from Aishah, something similar."
"102","106 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Simak, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that one of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bathed from major impurity, then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to perform ablution, and she said to him, so he said: Verily nothing renders water impure. So we have reported in these reports the purification of each of the man and the woman with the leftover of his companion, and that contradicted what we reported at the beginning of this chapter, so reflection was due here to extract thereby from the two contradictory meanings a sound meaning. We found the agreed-upon principle that when a man and a woman take water with their hands together from one vessel, that does not render the water impure. And we saw that all impurities, when they fall into water before performing ablution from it or along with performing ablution from it, their ruling is the same. Since that is so, and the ablution of each of the man and the woman along with his companion does not render the water impure for him, his ablution after him from his leftover is by reasoning likewise. Thereby what the other party held is established, and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on them."
"103","5 - Chapter: naming Allah over ablution. 107 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Wuhayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah narrated to us that he heard Abu Thifal al-Murri say: I heard Rabah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sufyan ibn Huwaytib say: My grandmother narrated to me that she heard Abu Hurayrah say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There is no prayer for one who has no ablution, and no ablution for one who does not mention the name of Allah over it."
"104","108 - Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to me, from Abu Thifal al-Murri, he said: I heard Rabah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Sufyan say: My grandmother narrated to me that she heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say that."
"105","109 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi informed us, from Ibn Harmalah, from Abu Thifal al-Murri, from Rabah ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Amiri, from Ibn Thawban, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), something similar. A group of people held that whoever does not name Allah over the ablution for prayer, his ablution does not suffice him, and they argued for that with these reports. Others disagreed with them and said: Whoever does not name Allah over his ablution has done wrong, yet he has become pure with that ablution of his. And they argued for that with that which [follows]:"
"106","110 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, from Said, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Hudayn Abu Sasan, from al-Muhajir ibn Qunfudh, that he greeted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was performing ablution, and he did not return it to him; when he finished his ablution he said: Nothing prevented me from returning it to you except that I disliked mentioning Allah except in a state of purity. So in this hadith is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked mentioning Allah except in a state of purity, and he returned the greeting after the ablution by which he became purified. In that is proof that he had performed ablution before mentioning the name of Allah. And his saying: No ablution for one who does not name Allah, also bears what the adherents of the first view said, and bears: No ablution for him, i.e. no complete ablution for him in reward, as he said: The poor is not the one whom a date or two dates and a morsel or two morsels turn away. By that it was not intended that he is not a poor one altogether out of the bounds of poverty until charity becomes forbidden for him. Rather by that it was intended that he is not the fully poor one in poverty, than whose degree in poverty there is no degree."
"107","111 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdillah narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Hajari, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdillah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: The poor is not the beggar whom a date or two dates and a morsel or two morsels turn away. They said: So who is the poor? He said: The one who is shy to ask, and does not find what suffices him, and is not noticed so he is given."
"108","112 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Qabisah ibn Uqbah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibrahim, then he mentioned something similar with its chain."
"109","113 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhuayb informed us, from Abu al-Walid, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar to it."
"110","114 - Abu Umayyah Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ayyash al-Himsi narrated to us, from Ibn Thawban, from Abdillah ibn al-Fadl, from Abd al-Rahman al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), something similar."
"111","115 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), something similar, or as he said: The believer is not the one who spends the night satiated while his neighbor is hungry."
"112","116 - Abu Bakrah narrated that to us, he said: Muammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bashir, from Abdillah ibn al-Musawir or Ibn Abi al-Musawir, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas reproaching Ibn al-Zubayr about miserliness and saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The believer is not the one who spends the night satiated while his neighbor beside him is hungry. By that he did not intend that he is not a believer with a belief by abandoning which he exits to disbelief, but he intended thereby that he is not at the highest ranks of belief; and the likes of this are many, the book would be lengthy by mentioning them. Likewise his saying: No ablution for one who does not name Allah, he did not intend thereby that he is not one performing ablution with which he has not exited from ritual impurity, but he intended that he is not one performing a complete ablution among the causes of ablution that necessitate reward. Since this hadith bears of meanings what we described, and there was no decisive proof for one of the two interpretations over the other, its meaning had to be made agreeable with the meanings of the hadith of al-Muhajir, so they do not contradict. Thereby it is established that ablution without naming exits the performer from ritual impurity to purity. As for the aspect of that by way of reasoning, we have seen things that are not entered into except with speech. Among them are the contracts that people conclude for one another, of sales, leases, marriages, divorce by compensation, and what resembles that. Those things are not effected except with sayings, and the sayings thereof are effecting, for he says: I have sold you, I have married you to her, I have divorced you by compensation. Those are sayings containing mention of the contracts. And there are things entered into with sayings, namely prayer and pilgrimage; one enters prayer with the takbir, and pilgrimage with the talbiyah. So the takbir in prayer and the talbiyah in pilgrimage were a pillar of their pillars. Then we returned to naming over ablution: does it resemble anything of that? We saw no effecting of anything mentioned therein as there was in marriage and sales. So naming thereby exited from the ruling of what we laid down, nor was naming a pillar of the pillars of ablution as the takbir was a pillar of the pillars of prayer, and as the talbiyah was a pillar of the pillars of pilgrimage; thereby its ruling also exited from the ruling of the takbir and the talbiyah. Thereby the saying of one who said that it is indispensable in ablution, as those things are indispensable in what is done therein, is nullified. If a sayer says: We have seen that a slaughtered animal indispensably requires naming at it, and whoever abandons that deliberately, his slaughter is not eaten, so naming over ablution is likewise. It is said to him: It has not been established in the ruling of reasoning that whoever abandons naming over a slaughtered animal deliberately, it is not eaten; people have disputed in that. Some said: It is eaten, and some said: It is not eaten. As for one who said it is eaten, we have been sufficed the explanation for his saying. As for one who said it is not eaten, he says: If he abandons it forgetfully, it is eaten, and it is the same according to him whether the slaughterer is a Muslim or a disbeliever, so long as he is of the People of the Book. So naming here, in the saying of one who obliged it in slaughter, is only for clarifying the religion. When the slaughterer names, his slaughter becomes of the slaughters of the eaten religion whose slaughter is eaten, and when he does not name, it is made of the slaughters of the religions whose slaughters are not eaten. And naming over ablution is not for religion; rather it is made for remembrance upon a cause of the causes of prayer. We saw among the causes of prayer ablution and covering the private parts; so whoever covered his private parts without naming, that did not harm him. By reasoning upon that, whoever purified himself also without naming, that did not harm him. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on them."
"113","6 - Chapter: ablution for prayer once each and thrice each. 117 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Zaidah ibn Qudamah narrated to us, he said: Alqamah ibn Khalid, or Khalid ibn Alqamah, narrated to us, from Abd Khayr, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that he performed ablution thrice, then said: This is the purification of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"114","118 - Husayn narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Abu Hayyah al-Wadii, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), something similar."
"115","119 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Thawban informed us, from Abdah ibn Abi Lubabah, from Shaqiq, he said: I saw Ali and Uthman perform ablution thrice each, and both said: Thus would the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform ablution."
"116","120 - Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Suri narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, he said: Ibn Thawban narrated to us, then he mentioned something similar with its chain."
"117","121 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abd al-Majid al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Abdillah, from Abdillah ibn Jafar, from Uthman ibn Affan, that he performed ablution thrice each and said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform ablution thus."
"118","122 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Subay, from Abu Umamah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ablution thrice each. So in these reports is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ablution thrice each, and it has been reported from him that he performed ablution once each."
"119","123 - al-Rabi ibn Sulayman al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, he said: al-Dahhak ibn Shurahbil narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform ablution once each."
"120","124 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Shall I not inform you of the ablution of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), once each, or he said: He performed ablution once each."
"121","125 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Abdillah ibn Umar, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ablution once each."
"122","126 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Umarah, from Ibn Abi Najih, then he mentioned something similar with its chain."
"123","127 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, both said: Said ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, from Abdillah ibn Ubaydillah ibn Abi Rafi, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform ablution thrice each, and I saw him wash once each. So it is established by what we mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he performed ablution once each; thereby it is established that what was of his ablution thrice each was only for attaining excellence, not obligation."
"124","7 - Chapter: obligation of wiping the head in ablution. 128 - Yunus, Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil, and Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdillah ibn Salim and Malik ibn Anas informed me, from Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini, from his father, from Abdillah ibn Zayd ibn Asim al-Mazini, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he took with his hand in his ablution for prayer water, and began with the front of his head, then went with his hand to the back of the head, then returned them to its front. Malik said: This is the best I heard in that, and the most general in wiping the head."
"125","129 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: My father and Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, from Layth, from Talhah ibn Musarrif, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wipe the front of his head until he reached the nape, the back of the head, from the front of his neck."
"126","130 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Mamar narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith ibn Said narrated to us, from Layth, then he mentioned something similar with its chain."
"127","131 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Bahr narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn al-Ala narrated to us, from Abu al-Azhar, from Muawiyah, that he showed them the ablution of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When he reached wiping the head, he placed his palms upon the front of his head, then passed them until he reached the nape, then returned them until he reached the place from which he began. Some holders held that wiping the whole head is obligatory in the ablution for prayer, and abandoning any of it does not suffice, and they argued for that with these reports. Others disagreed with them and said: What is in these reports of yours is only that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped his whole head in his ablution for prayer; thus we order one performing ablution to do that in his ablution for prayer, but we do not oblige that in its entirety upon him as duty. And there is nothing in the act of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) doing it that has indicated that was from him because it is duty, for we have seen him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform ablution thrice each, not that that is duty than which less does not suffice, but of it is duty and of it is excellence. And there has been reported from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of the reports indicating what they held about duty in wiping the head being upon part of it that which [follows]."
"128","132 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Amr ibn Wahb al-Thaqafi, from al-Mughirah ibn Shubah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ablution while wearing a turban, so he wiped over his turban and wiped his forelock."
"129","133 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun say: Ibn Awn informed us, from Amir, from Ibn al-Mughirah ibn Shubah, from his father, and Ibn Awn, from Ibn Sirin, from Amr ibn Wahb, from al-Mughirah, raising it to him, he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey, and he performed ablution for prayer, so he wiped over his turban, and he mentioned the forelock with something. So in this report is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped part of the head, namely the forelock, and the appearance of the forelock is proof that the rest of the head has the ruling of what appeared of it; for had the ruling been established by wiping over the turban, it would have been like wiping over the two leather socks, in which the two feet must have been concealed therein. Had part of the two feet been exposed, it would not have sufficed him to wash what appeared of them and wipe over what was concealed of them; rather the ruling of what was concealed of them was made attached to the ruling of what appeared of them. Since washing the apparent became obligatory, washing the concealed became obligatory. Likewise the head: since wiping what appeared of it became obligatory, it is established that it is not permissible to wipe what is concealed of it, so that the ruling of all of it is one ruling, as the ruling of the two feet, when part of them is concealed in the leather socks, was one ruling. Since the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sufficed in this report with wiping the forelock over wiping the rest of the head, that indicated that the duty in wiping the head is the measure of the forelock, and that what he did beyond the forelock in the other reports was proof of excellence, not obligation, so that these reports agree and do not contradict. This is the ruling of this chapter by way of reports. As for by way of reasoning, we have seen that ablution is obligatory in limbs. Of them is what has the ruling of being washed, and of them is what has the ruling of being wiped. As for what has the ruling of being washed, it is the face, the two hands, and the two feet, in the saying of one who obliges washing them. All have agreed that what must be washed thereof must all be washed, and washing part of it to the exclusion of part does not suffice. And whenever there was what must be wiped thereof, namely the head, one group said: Its ruling is that all of it be wiped, as those limbs are all washed, and others said: Part of it is wiped to the exclusion of part. So we looked at how the ruling of wiping is. We saw that the ruling of wiping over the two leather socks has been disputed. One group said: Their outer is wiped to the exclusion of their inner, and others said: Their outer and inner are wiped. All have agreed that the duty of wiping therein is upon part of them to the exclusion of wiping all of them. By reasoning upon that, the ruling of wiping the head is likewise: upon part of it to the exclusion of part, by analogy and reasoning, upon what we explained of that. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah have mercy on them. And there has been reported in that from those after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also that which agrees with that."
"130","134 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us, from al-Zubaydi, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, that he used to wipe the front of his head when he performed ablution."
"131","8 - Chapter: ruling of the two ears in the ablution for prayer. 135 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Kurayb Muhammad ibn al-Ala narrated to us, he said: Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Talhah ibn Yazid ibn Rukanah, from Ubaydullah al-Khawlani, from Abdillah ibn Abbas, he said: Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, came to me after pouring out water, and called for a vessel containing water and said: O Ibn Abbas, shall I not perform ablution for you as I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform ablution? I said: Yes, may my father and mother be ransom for you. Then he mentioned a long hadith in which he mentioned that he took a handful of water with both his hands together and struck his face with them, then the second likewise, then the third, then he inserted his thumbs into what faces of his two ears, then he took a handful of water with his right hand and poured it upon his forelock, then let it flow over his face, then washed his right hand to the elbow thrice and the left likewise, then wiped his head and the backs of his ears. A group of people adopted this report and said: What faces of the two ears has the ruling of the face, washed with the face, and what is behind of them has the ruling of the head, wiped with the head. Others disagreed with them and said: The two ears are of the head; their front and back are wiped with the head. And they argued for that with that which [follows]."
"132","136 - With what Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Amir ibn Shaqiq, from Shaqiq ibn Salamah, from Uthman ibn Affan, that he performed ablution and wiped his head and his ears, their outer and inner, and said: Thus I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform ablution."
"133","137 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Sayrafi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: al-Darawardi narrated to us, he said: Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to us, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ablution and wiped his head and his ears."
"134","138 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, then he mentioned something similar with its chain, except that he said: Once."
"135","139 - Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Maymun al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hariz ibn Uthman narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Maysarah, that he heard al-Miqdam ibn Madikarib say: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform ablution; when he reached wiping the head, he placed his palms upon the front of his head, then passed them until he reached the nape, then returned them until he reached the place from which he began, and wiped his ears, their outer and inner, once."
"136","140 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah informed us, from Abu al-Aswad, from Abbad ibn Tamim al-Ansari, from his father, that he saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform ablution and wipe his head and his ears, their inner and outer."
"137","141 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muadh narrated to us, he said: Ubayy narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Habib al-Ansari narrated to us, Ibn Abi Dawud said, and he is Habib ibn Zayd, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from Abdillah ibn Zayd, the grandfather of this Habib; he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) brought ablution water, and he rubbed his ears when he wiped them."
"138","142 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Musa ibn Abi Aishah, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that a man came to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: How is purification? So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for water and performed ablution, and inserted his two forefingers into his ears, and wiped the outer of his ears with his thumbs and the inner of his ears with his forefingers."
"139","143 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Sinan ibn Rabiah, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abu Umamah al-Bahili, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ablution and wiped his ears with the head, and said: The two ears are of the head."
"140","144 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ajlan narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from al-Rubayyi bint Muawwidh ibn Afra, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ablution in her presence and wiped his head along the courses of the hair, and wiped his temples and his ears, their outer and inner."
"141","145 - Ibrahim ibn Munqidh al-Usfuri narrated to us, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ajlan narrated to me, then he mentioned something similar with its chain."
"142","146 - Abu al-Awwam Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Abd al-Jabbar al-Muradi narrated to us, he said: My uncle Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to me, from Ibn Ajlan, then he mentioned something similar with its chain."
"143","147 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ajlan narrated to us, then he mentioned something similar with its chain."
"144","148 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Abdillah ibn Muhammad, from al-Rubayyi, she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us and performed ablution, and wiped the outer of his ears and their inner."
"145","149 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Muhammad, from al-Rubayyi, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), something similar. Abu Jafar said: So in these reports is that the ruling of the two ears, what faces of them and what is behind, is of the head, and reports have successively come with that as they have not successively come with what contradicts it. This is the aspect of this chapter by way of reports. As for by way of reasoning, we have seen them not differing that a woman in pilgrim sanctity may not cover her face, while she may cover her head, and all have agreed that she may cover her ears, their outer and inner. That indicated that their ruling is the ruling of the head in wiping, not the ruling of the face. Another proof is that we have seen them not differing that what is behind of them is wiped with the head, while they differed about what faces of them upon what we mentioned. So we looked into that and saw the limbs upon whose obligatoriness in ablution they agreed: the face, the two hands, the two feet, and the head. The face is all washed, likewise the two hands, likewise the two feet, and the ruling of nothing of those limbs was contrary to the ruling of its rest. Rather the ruling of each limb thereof was made one ruling, so it was made all washed or all wiped. And they agreed that what is behind of the two ears has the ruling of wiping, so by reasoning upon that, what faces of them is likewise, and the ruling of the two ears is all one ruling, as the ruling of the other limbs we mentioned was. This is the aspect of reasoning in this chapter, and it is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. And a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) have said that."
"146","150 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Humayd, he said: I saw Anas ibn Malik perform ablution, and he wiped his ears, their outer and inner sides, along with his head, and he said: Ibn Masud used to enjoin (wiping) the two ears."
"147","151 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, he said: Humayd narrated to me, and he mentioned the like of it."
"148","152 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abu Hamzah, he said: I saw Ibn Abbas perform ablution, and he wiped his ears, their outer and inner sides. So here is Ibn Abbas, who had narrated from Ali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what we already narrated at the beginning of this chapter; and Ata ibn Yasar narrated from him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), as we narrated in the second section of this chapter; then he himself acted upon that and abandoned what Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, had narrated to him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So this is evidence that the abrogation of what was narrated from Ali had become established with him."
"149","153 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that he used to say: The two ears are part of the head, so wipe them."
"150","154 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Ghaylan ibn Abdillah, he said: I heard Ibn Umar saying: The two ears are part of the head."
"151","155 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: Ayyub narrated to us, from Nafi, that Ibn Umar used to wipe his ears, their outer and inner sides, tracing the folds with that."
"152","9 - Chapter on the obligation of the two feet in the ablution for prayer. 156 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, from al-Nazzal ibn Sabrah, he said: I saw Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, pray Zuhr, then sit for the people in the courtyard, then water was brought, and he wiped his face and his hands, and wiped his head and his feet, and drank the remainder standing, then said: Some people claim that this is disliked, but I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do the like of what I did. And this is the ablution of one who has not broken his ablution. Abu Jafar said: In our view there is no proof in this hadith that the obligation of the two feet is wiping, because it mentions that he wiped his face, yet that wiping was washing; so it is possible that his wiping of his feet was likewise."
"153","157 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Abdah narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Talhah ibn Yazid ibn Rukanah, from Ubaydullah al-Khawlani, from Ibn Abbas, he said: He entered upon Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, after he had poured out water, and he called for ablution water, so we brought him a vessel of water, and he said: O Ibn Abbas, shall I perform ablution for you as I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform ablution? I said: Yes, may my father and mother be ransom for you. Then he mentioned a long hadith. He said: Then he took with both his hands together a handful of water and struck it upon his right foot, and likewise the left."
"154","158 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ablution, and he took a handful of water and sprinkled it upon his feet while he was wearing sandals."
"155","159 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from al-Suddi, from Abd Khayr, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that he performed ablution and wiped over the top of the foot, and said: Were it not that I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do it, the bottom of the foot would be more deserving than the top."
"156","160 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn al-Husayn al-Luhbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhib, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, that when he performed ablution with his sandals on his feet, he would wipe the tops of his feet with his hands, and would say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do like this."
"157","161 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Talhah informed us, he said: Ali ibn Yahya ibn Khallad narrated to us, from his father, from his uncle Rifaah ibn Rafi, that he was sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the hadith, until he said: The prayer of none of you is complete until he performs ablution thoroughly as Allah, Mighty and Majestic, commanded him: so he washes his face and his hands up to the elbows, and wipes his head and his feet up to the ankles."
"158","162 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Abu al-Aswad, from Abbad ibn Tamim, from his uncle, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ablution and wiped over the two feet, and that Urwah used to do that. So some people adopted this and said: Such is the ruling of the two feet, they are wiped as the head is wiped. Others disagreed with them on that and said: Rather they are washed; and they cited as proof from the reports what follows."
"159","163 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Zaidah ibn Qudamah narrated to us, he said: Alqamah ibn Khalid - or Khalid ibn Alqamah - narrated to us, from Abd Khayr, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, entered the courtyard, then said to his servant: Bring me purification water. So he brought him water and a basin, and he performed ablution and washed his feet three times each, and said: Such was the purification of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"160","164 - Husayn narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Abu Hayyah al-Wadii, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"161","165 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"162","166 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Malik ibn Urfutah, he said: I heard Abd Khayr say: I heard Ali, and he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"163","167 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Abd al-Majid narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Abdillah, from Abdillah ibn Jafar, from Uthman ibn Affan, that he performed ablution and washed his feet three times each, and said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform ablution like this."
"164","168 - Yunus and Ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, they said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, that Ata ibn Yazid al-Laythi informed him that Humran, the freedman of Uthman, informed him, from Uthman, the like of it."
"165","169 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Safwan ibn Isa narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdillah ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: I entered the house of Zayd ibn Darah, and he heard me rinsing my mouth, and said to me: O Abu Muhammad. I said: At your service. He said: Shall I inform you of the ablution of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? I said: Yes. He said: I saw Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, at the seats; he called for ablution water and performed ablution three times each, and washed his feet three times, then said: Whoever wishes to look at the ablution of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), let him look at my ablution."
"166","170 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, he said: Kathir ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: al-Muttalib ibn Abdillah ibn Hantab al-Makhzumi narrated to us, from Humran ibn Aban, that Uthman performed ablution and washed his feet three times each, and said: If I said that this is the ablution of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I would have spoken the truth."
"167","171 - Ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Ibn Lahiah informed me, from Yazid ibn Amr al-Maafiri, he said: I heard Abu Abd al-Rahman Abdillah ibn Yazid saying: I heard al-Mustawrid ibn Shaddad al-Qurashi saying: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rubbing with his little finger what is between the toes of his feet. And this can only occur in washing, because wiping does not reach that; it is only upon the tops of the two feet in particular."
"168","172 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Said ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, from Abdillah ibn Ubaydillah ibn Abi Rafi, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform ablution, and he washed his feet three times."
"169","174 - Yunus and Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, they said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from al-Rubayyi, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to come to us and perform ablution for prayer, washing his feet three times each."
"170","175 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Amir al-Ahwal narrated to us, from Ata, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ablution: he rinsed his mouth and nose three times, washed his face three times, and his forearms three times each, wiped his head, and washed his feet."
"171","176 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Musa ibn Abi Aishah, from Amr ibn Shuayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him: How is purification? So he called for water, and performed ablution three times each, and wiped his head, and washed his feet, then said: Such is ablution; whoever adds to this or subtracts has done wrong and acted unjustly."
"172","177 - Yunus and Ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, they said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini, from his father, that he said to Abdillah ibn Zayd ibn Asim: Are you able to show me how the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform ablution? So he called for water, and performed ablution, and washed his feet."
"173","178 - Bahr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from his father, that Abu Jubayr al-Kindi came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he ordered ablution water for him, and said: Perform ablution, O Abu Jubayr. So he began with his mouth, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Do not begin with your mouth, for the disbeliever begins with his mouth. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for water, and performed ablution three times each, then wiped his head and washed his feet."
"174","179 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Adam narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sad narrated to us, from Muawiyah, then he mentioned the like of it with its chain. Fahd said: So I mentioned it to Abdillah ibn Salih, and he said: I heard it from Muawiyah ibn Salih. So these reports have successively come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he washed his feet in his ablution for prayer; and there has also been narrated from him what indicates that their ruling is washing."
"175","180 - Among what was narrated on that is what Yunus and Ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, they said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the Muslim or believing servant performs ablution and washes his face, every sin he looked at with his eye exits from his face; and when he washes his hands, every sin his hands seized exits from his hands; and when he washes his feet, every sin his feet walked to exits."
"176","181 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Yaqub informed us, he said: Abbad ibn Abi Salih al-Samman narrated to me that he heard his father saying: I heard Abu Hurayrah saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There is no Muslim who performs ablution and washes the whole of his feet, except every evil deed they walked to exits along with the dripping of the water."
"177","182 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Rabi narrated to us, from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Thalabah ibn Abbad al-Abdi, from his father, he said: What informs you - he narrated it to me - the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in pairs and singly: There is no servant who performs ablution and performs it well, washing his face until water flows upon his chin, then washing his forearms until water flows upon his elbows, and washing his feet until water flows from before his ankles, then standing and praying two rakahs, except he is forgiven what preceded of his sin."
"178","183 - Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh al-Basri narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid said: Qays narrated to us, and he mentioned the like of it with its chain."
"179","184 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj al-Hadrami narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Shurahbil ibn al-Simt, that he said: Who will narrate to us from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? So Amr ibn Abasah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When a man calls for his purification water and washes his face, his sins fall from his face and the edges of his beard; and when he washes his hands, his sins fall from the tips of his fingers; and when he wipes his head, his sins fall from the ends of his hair; and when he washes his feet, the sins of his feet exit from the soles of his feet."
"180","185 - Bahr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Muawiyah ibn Salih narrated to me, from Damrah ibn Habib and Abu Yahya and Abu Talhah, from Abu Umamah al-Bahili, from Amr ibn Abasah, he said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, how is ablution? He said: When you perform ablution and wash your hands three times, your sins exit from between your nails and fingertips; and when you rinse your mouth and nose in your nostrils, and wash your face and your forearms up to the elbows, and wash your feet up to the ankles, you are washed of most of your sins. So these reports also indicate that the two feet, their obligation is washing; for had their obligation been wiping, there would be no reward in washing them. Do you not see that the head, whose obligation is wiping, has no reward in washing it; so since there is reward in washing the two feet, that proves that their obligation is washing. And there has also been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates that."
"181","186 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Said ibn Abi Karib, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw a spot on a man's foot that he had not washed, and said: Woe to the heels from the Fire."
"182","187 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Said ibn Abi Kurayb, from Jabir, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Woe to the heels from the Fire; perform ablution thoroughly."
"183","188 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Umar ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to me, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to me, he said: Abu Salamah narrated to us, he said: Salim, the freedman of al-Mahri, narrated to us, he said: I heard Aishah calling Abd al-Rahman: Perform ablution thoroughly, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Woe to the heels from the Fire."
"184","189 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ajlan narrated to us, from al-Maqburi, from Abu Salamah, that he heard Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, saying: O Abd al-Rahman, and he mentioned the like of it."
"185","190 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Salim al-Dawsi, from Aishah, the like of it."
"186","191 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Zarah narrated to us, he said: Haywah ibn Shurayh informed us, he said: Abu al-Aswad informed us, that Abu Abdillah, the freedman of Shaddad ibn al-Had, narrated to him that he entered upon Aishah, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with her being Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr, then he mentioned the like of it."
"187","192 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Bilal informed us, he said: Suhayl ibn Abi Salih narrated to me, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Woe to the heels from the Fire on the Day of Resurrection."
"188","193 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ziyad, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: Abu al-Qasim, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: Woe to the heels from the Fire."
"189","194 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn al-Jad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, and he mentioned the like of it with its chain."
"190","195 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdillah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, from Haywah ibn Shurayh, from Uqbah ibn Muslim, from Abdillah ibn al-Harith ibn Jaz al-Zubaydi, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Woe to the heels and the soles of the feet from the Fire."
"191","196 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: al-Layth and Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, they said: Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us, from Uqbah ibn Muslim, he said: I heard Abdillah ibn al-Harith ibn Jaz saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, and he mentioned the like of it."
"192","197 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Zaidah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Abu Yahya, from Abdillah ibn Amr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Woe to the heels from the Fire."
"193","198 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Abu Yahya, from Abdillah ibn Amr, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw people who had performed ablution as if they had left something of their feet, and said: Woe to the heels from the Fire; perform ablution thoroughly."
"194","199 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Zaidah informed us, from Mansur, from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Abu Yahya, from Abdillah ibn Amr, he said: We traveled with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Makkah to Madinah, and he came upon water between Makkah and Madinah; Asr arrived, some people went ahead, and we reached them while they had performed ablution with their heels gleaming untouched by water. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Woe to the heels from the Fire; perform ablution thoroughly."
"195","200 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Yusuf ibn Mahak, from Abdillah ibn Amr, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stayed behind us in a journey we traveled, and caught up with us when the Asr prayer had pressed us while we were performing ablution and wiping over our feet; so Bilal called out: Woe to the heels from the Fire, two or three times."
"196","201 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, and he mentioned the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So Abdillah ibn Amr mentioned that they were wiping until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them to perform ablution thoroughly and warned them, saying: Woe to the heels from the Fire. So that proves that the ruling of the wiping they were doing was abrogated by what came after it of what we mentioned. So this is the ruling of this chapter by way of reports. As for its aspect by way of reasoning, we already mentioned earlier in this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what reward belongs to one who washes his feet in his ablution; so it is thereby established that they are among what is washed, and that they are not like the head which is wiped, whose washer has no reward in washing it. And what is established by these reports is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. And people differed over His saying, Exalted: {and your feet}: some joined it to His saying, Exalted: {wipe your heads}, restricting it to the meaning: wipe your heads and your feet; and some joined it to His saying: {wash your faces and your hands up to the elbows}, reading {and your feet} in coordination with His saying: wash your faces and wash your hands and wash your feet, by ellipsis and coordination. And the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and those after them differed on that. Among what was narrated from them on that is what follows."
"197","202 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Qays, from Asim, from Zirr, that Abdillah ibn Masud read {and your feet} with a fathah."
"198","203 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith ibn Said and Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that he read it likewise."
"199","204 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yaqub narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Yusuf ibn Mihran, from Ibn Abbas, the like of it."
"200","205 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: I heard Hushaym saying: Khalid al-Hadhdha informed us, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that he read it likewise and said: It returns to washing."
"201","206 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yaqub narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Qays, from Mujahid, he said: The Quran returns to washing, and he read: {and your feet}, pronouncing it with nasb."
"202","207 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"203","208 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yaqub narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, the like of it."
"204","209 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yaqub narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Tayyah narrated to us, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, the like of it."
"205","210 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yaqub narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Asim, from al-Shabi, he said: The Quran came down with wiping, and the Sunnah with washing."
"206","211 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yaqub narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Araj narrated to us, from Mujahid, that he read it: {and your feet}, pronouncing it with khafd."
"207","212 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from Qurrah, from al-Hasan, that he read it likewise. And it has been narrated from a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that they used to wash. Among what was narrated on that is what follows."
"208","213 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zubayr ibn Adi, from Ibrahim, he said: I said to al-Aswad: Did Umar used to wash his feet? He said: Yes, he used to wash them thoroughly."
"209","214 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: Umar performed ablution and washed his feet."
"210","215 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abu Rabiah narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Jamrah, he said: I saw Ibn Abbas washing his feet three times each."
"211","216 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahiah informed us, from Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah, from Ibn al-Mujammar, he said: I saw Abu Hurayrah perform ablution once, and when he washed his forearms he would almost reach half the upper arm, and his feet to half the shin. So I said to him about that, and he said: I wish to extend my radiance, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: My nation will come on the Day of Resurrection bright-faced and bright-limbed from ablution, and none of the nations will come like that."
"212","217 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Yaqub narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Mujahid, that wiping over the two feet was mentioned to him, and he said: Ibn Umar used to wash his feet thoroughly, and I would pour water upon him."
"213","218 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, the like of it."
"214","219 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdillah al-Majishun narrated to us, from Abdillah ibn Dinar, from Ibn Umar, that he used to wash his feet when he performed ablution."
"215","220 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Salam narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik, he said: I said to Ata: Has it reached you from any of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he wiped the two feet? He said: No. And one who claimed has claimed that reasoning necessitates wiping the two feet in the ablution for prayer. He said: Because I saw their ruling more resembling the ruling of the head, for I saw that when water is unavailable and its obligation becomes tayammum, one wipes the face and hands and does not wipe the head nor the feet. So since absence of water drops the obligation of washing the face and hands to another obligation, namely tayammum, and drops the obligation of the head and feet not to a substitute, that proves that the ruling of the two feet in the presence of water is like the ruling of the head, not like the ruling of the face and hands. So part of the proof against him in that is that we see things whose obligation is washing in the presence of water, then that obligation drops in the absence of water not to a substitute: among them the junub, who must wash his whole body with water when present, but if water is unavailable, tayammum in the face and hands becomes obligatory upon him. So the obligation of the rest of his body after the face and hands is dropped not to a substitute, yet that was not proof that what dropped not to a substitute had wiping as its obligation in the presence of water. Likewise, the dropping of the obligation of the two feet in the absence of water not to a substitute is not proof that their ruling in the presence of water was wiping. So the opponent's cause is thereby nullified, since he is bound in his saying by the like of what he bound his disputant to."
"216","10 - Chapter: Is ablution obligatory for every prayer or not? 221 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Sulayman ibn Buraydah, from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to perform ablution for every prayer, but when the Conquest came he prayed the prayers with a single ablution."
"217","222 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Asim and Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, from Sulayman ibn Buraydah, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed on the day of the conquest of Makkah five prayers with one ablution, and wiped over his khuffs. So Umar said to him: You have done something, O Messenger of Allah, that you did not used to do. He said: I did it deliberately, O Umar."
"218","223 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Alqamah narrated to us, from Sulayman, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he used to perform ablution for every prayer. So some people held that those present must perform ablution for every prayer, and cited this hadith as proof. Most scholars disagreed with them, saying: Ablution is only obligatory from hadath."
"219","224 - Among what was narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that, agreeing with what they held, is what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd, Ibn Jurayj, and Ibn Saman informed me, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abdillah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went to a woman of the Ansar with his Companions, and she brought near to them a roasted sheep, and he ate and we ate; then Zuhr came, so he performed ablution and prayed; then he returned to the remainder of his food and ate; then Asr came, and he prayed without performing ablution. Abu Jafar said: In this hadith he prayed Zuhr and Asr with his ablution from the time of Zuhr. And it is possible that his ablution for every prayer, as Ibn Buraydah narrated, was seeking excellence, not obligation."
"220","225 - If one who says asks: Is there excellence in this to be sought? It is said to him: Yes. Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn Anum informed me, from Abu Ghutayf al-Hudhali, he said: I prayed Zuhr with Abdillah ibn Umar ibn al-Khattab, then he withdrew to a sitting place in his house, and I withdrew with him, until when the call for Asr came he called for ablution water and performed ablution; then he went out, and I went out with him, and prayed Asr; then he returned to his sitting place, and I returned with him, until when the call for Maghrib came he called for ablution water and performed ablution. So I said to him: What is this, O Abu Abd al-Rahman, ablution at every prayer? He said: Have you noticed this of me? It is not a sunnah; my ablution for the dawn prayer would suffice for all my prayers, so long as I do not break it; but I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: Whoever performs ablution upon purity, Allah writes for him thereby ten good deeds. So for that I desired, O son of my brother. So it is possible that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only did what Ibn Buraydah narrated of him to attain this excellence, not because it was obligatory upon him. And Anas ibn Malik also narrated what indicates what we mentioned."
"221","226 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Amir, from Anas ibn Malik, he said: Ablution water was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he performed ablution from it. So I said to Anas: Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perform ablution at every prayer? He said: Yes. I said: And you? He said: We used to pray the prayers with one ablution. So here is Anas, who knew the ruling of what we mentioned of the act of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and did not deem it obligatory upon others. And it is also possible that he used to do that while it was obligatory, then it was abrogated; so we looked into that, whether we find anything among the reports indicating this meaning."
"222","227 - And behold, Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Abdillah ibn Abdillah ibn Umar, he said: I said to him: Do you see the ablution of Ibn Umar for every prayer, whether pure or not pure - upon what is that? He said: Asma, the daughter of Zayd ibn al-Khattab, narrated to me: Abdillah ibn Hanzalah ibn Abi Amir narrated to her: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was commanded to perform ablution for every prayer, whether pure or not pure; then when that became hard upon him, he was commanded to use the siwak for every prayer. And Ibn Umar deemed that he had strength for that, so he would not omit ablution for every prayer. So in this hadith the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was commanded to perform ablution for every prayer, then that was abrogated. So it is established by what we mentioned that ablution suffices so long as there is no hadath. If one who says says: In this hadith is the obligation of the siwak for every prayer, so how do you not make that obligatory, nor act upon the whole hadith, since you acted upon part of it? It is said to him: It is possible that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was singled out with the siwak for every prayer apart from his nation. And it is possible that they and he are alike in that, and its reality cannot be reached except by revelation. So we considered that, whether we find anything therein indicating any of that."
"223","228 - And behold, Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, he said: my uncle Abd al-Rahman ibn Yasar narrated to me, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Rafi, from his father, from Ali, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it not that I would impose hardship upon my nation, I would have commanded them to use the siwak at every prayer."
"224","229 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman, he said: Abdillah ibn Yasar narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: The Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated to us, from the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of that."
"225","230 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Khalaf al-Tufawi narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Jafar said: This is a strange hadith; we did not write it except from Ibn Marzuq."
"226","231 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Zayd ibn Khalid, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"227","232 - Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Yaqub narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, he said: Said al-Maqburi narrated to me, from Ata, the freedman of Umm Subayyah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"228","233 - Yunus and Ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, they said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it not that I would impose hardship upon my nation, I would have commanded them to use the siwak with every prayer."
"229","234 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Were it not that I would impose hardship upon my nation, I would have commanded them to use the siwak with every ablution."
"230","235 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Anas ibn Iyad informed us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Were it not that I would impose hardship upon my nation, I would have commanded them to use the siwak at every prayer."
"231","236 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us (h), 237 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Said al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"232","238 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, raising it, the like of it. So it is established by his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Were it not that I would impose hardship upon my nation, I would have commanded them to use the siwak at every prayer, that he did not command them with that, and that it is not upon them; and that in the lifting of that from them - which was made a substitute for ablution for every prayer - is proof that ablution for every prayer was not upon them nor were they commanded with it, and that the one commanded with it was the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) apart from them, and that his ruling in that was other than their ruling. So this is the aspect of this chapter by way of establishing the meanings of the reports. And the obligation of ablution for every prayer is thereby established as lifted. As for its aspect by way of reasoning: we saw ablution as purification from hadath, so we wished to look into purifications from hadaths, how is their ruling, and what nullifies them? We found the purifications made obligatory by hadaths to be of two kinds: among them ghusl, and among them ablution. So one who had intercourse or became junub was obliged to perform ghusl, and one who urinated or defecated was obliged to perform ablution. And the ghusl obligatory by what we mentioned is not nullified by the passing of times, nor is it nullified except by hadaths. So since it is established that the ruling of purification from intercourse and wet dreams is as we mentioned, then by reasoning likewise the ruling of purifications from the rest of the hadaths is so, and that the passing of a time does not nullify that, just as the passing of a time does not nullify ghusl. And another proof: we saw them agree that the traveler prays all prayers with one ablution so long as he does not break it. And they differed only over the resident. And we found the hadaths of intercourse, wet dreams, stool, and urine, and every hadath obligating purification - and this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah have mercy on them. And a group after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) held likewise."
"233","239 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, from Anas ibn Malik, that the companions of Abu Musa al-Ashari performed ablution and prayed Zuhr. When Asr arrived, they stood to perform ablution, so he said to them: What is with you, have you broken your ablution? They said: No. He said: Ablution without hadath - it is about to be that a man kills his father, his brother, his uncle, and his cousin, while performing ablution without hadath."
"234","240 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Amir, he said: I heard Anas saying: We used to pray all prayers with one ablution, so long as we did not break it."
"235","241 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Masud ibn Ali informed me, from Ikrimah, that Sad used to pray all prayers with one ablution, so long as he did not break it."
"236","242 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it, except that he did not mention Ikrimah; and he added: And Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, used to perform ablution for every prayer, and recite: {When you stand for prayer, wash your faces and your hands}. Abu Jafar said: In our view there is no proof in this verse for the obligation of ablution for every prayer, for it is possible that his saying that refers to standing while in hadath. Do you not see that they agreed that the ruling of the traveler is this, that ablution is not obligatory upon him until he breaks it. So since it is established that this is the ruling of the traveler in this verse, though addressed by it as the resident is addressed, it is established that the ruling of the resident therein is likewise. And Ibn al-Faghwa said: When they broke their ablution, they would not speak until they performed ablution, so this verse came down: {When you stand for prayer}, informing that that is only standing for prayer after hadath."
"237","243 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us another time, he said: Abd al-Samad and Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, they said: Shubah narrated to us, from Masud ibn Ali, with that, and he did not mention Ikrimah."
"238","244 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Muhammad, that Shurayh used to pray all prayers with one ablution."
"239","245 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Ibrahim, from al-Hasan, that he saw no harm in that, and Allah knows best."
"240","11 - Chapter: A man from whose penis madhy exits, what does he do? 246 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Umayyah ibn Bistam narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ata, from Iyas ibn Khalifah, from Rafi ibn Khadij, that Ali ordered Ammar to ask the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about madhy, and he said: He washes his private parts and performs ablution. Abu Jafar said: So some people held that washing the private parts is obligatory upon a man when he emits madhy and when he urinates; and they cited this report as proof. Others disagreed with them, saying: That was not from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as making washing the private parts obligatory; rather it was so that the madhy retracts and does not exit. They said: Among that is what Muslims are commanded in sacrificial animals that have milk, to sprinkle the udder with water, so that it retracts therein and does not exit. And successive reports have come with what indicates what they said."
"241","247 - Among that is what Ibn Abi Dawud and Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, they said: Amr ibn Muhammad al-Naqid narrated to us, he said: Abidah ibn Humayd narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, he said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I was a man who emitted much madhy, so I ordered a man to ask the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: In it is ablution."
"242","248 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym informed us, he said: al-Amash informed us, from Mundhir Abu Yala al-Thawri, from Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah, he said: I heard him narrating from his father, he said: I used to find madhy, so I ordered al-Miqdad to ask the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, being shy to ask him because his daughter was with me; so he asked him, and he said: Every stallion emits madhy; so if it is mani, then in it is ghusl, and if it is madhy, then in it is ablution."
"243","249 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Zaidah ibn Qudamah narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I was a man who emitted much madhy, and the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was with me, so I sent to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Perform ablution and wash it."
"244","250 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about madhy, and said: In it is ablution, and in mani is ghusl."
"245","251 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Hani ibn Hani, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I was a man who emitted much madhy, and when I emitted madhy I would perform ghusl; so I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: In it is ablution."
"246","252 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Israil informed us (h). 253 - And Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Israil narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"247","254 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Zaidah narrated to us, he said: al-Rukayn ibn al-Rabi al-Fazari narrated to us, from Husayn ibn Qabisah, from Ali, he said: I was a man who emitted much madhy, so I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: When you see madhy, perform ablution and wash your penis; and when you see mani, perform ghusl."
"248","255 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata, from Aaish ibn Anas, he said: I heard Ali upon the pulpit saying: I was a man who emitted much madhy, and I wished to ask the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), but I was shy before him because his daughter was under me; so I ordered Ammar, and he asked him, and he said: Ablution suffices for it. Abu Jafar said: Do you not see that when Ali mentioned what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made obligatory upon him in that, he mentioned the ablution for prayer. So it is thereby established that whatever besides the ablution for prayer he commanded, it was only for other than the meaning for which the ablution for prayer became obligatory. And Sahl ibn Hunayf narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what also indicates this."
"249","256 - Nasr ibn Marzuq and Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, they said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Said ibn Ubayd ibn al-Sabbaq, from his father, from Sahl ibn Hunayf, that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about madhy, and he said: In it is ablution. So he informed that what is obligatory in it is ablution, and that negates that anything besides ablution is upon him."
"250","257 - If one who says says: It has been narrated from Umar ibn al-Khattab what agrees with what the people of the first saying said, and he mentioned what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, he said: Sulayman al-Taymi informed us, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, that Salman ibn Rabiah al-Bahili married a woman of Banu Uqayl, and he would come to her and fondle her. So he asked Umar ibn al-Khattab about that, and he said: When you find fluid, wash your penis and your testicles, and perform your ablution for prayer. It is said to him: It is possible that its aspect is also what we turned the aspect of the hadith of Rafi ibn Khadij to. And there has been narrated from a group after him what agrees with that."
"251","258 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Muammal ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us (h). 259 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Hilal ibn Yahya ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, both from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Mawraq al-Ijli, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: It is mani, madhy, and wady. As for madhy and wady, he washes his penis and performs ablution; and as for mani, in it is ghusl."
"252","260 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Jamrah, he said: I said to Ibn Abbas: I ride the mount and emit madhy. He said: Wash your penis and perform your ablution for prayer. Do you not see that when Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, mentioned what is obligatory in madhy, he mentioned ablution in particular, and when he commanded Abu Jamrah, he commanded him along with ablution to wash the penis."
"253","261 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: al-Rabi ibn Subayh narrated to us, from al-Hasan, concerning madhy and wady, he said: He washes his private part and performs his ablution for prayer."
"254","262 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Fayyad, from Said ibn Jubayr, he said: When a man emits madhy, he washes the glans and performs his ablution for prayer. Abu Jafar said: So this is the aspect of this chapter by way of establishing the meanings of the reports; what we described is thereby established. As for its aspect by way of reasoning: we saw the exit of madhy as hadath, so we wished to look into the exit of hadaths, what becomes obligatory thereby? The exit of stool obligates washing what of the body it touched, and does not obligate washing other than that except purification for prayer. Likewise the exit of blood from wherever it exits, in the saying of one who deems that hadath. So by reasoning upon that, likewise the exit of madhy, which is hadath, obligates no washing except the spot of the body it touched, besides purification for prayer. So that is also established by what we mentioned by way of reasoning. And this is the saying of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah, Exalted, have mercy on them."
"255","12 - Chapter: The ruling of mani, is it pure or impure? 263 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, from Hammam ibn al-Harith, that he was staying with Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and had a wet dream; and a slave girl of Aishah saw him washing the trace of janabah from his garment, or washing his garment; so she informed Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, of that. So Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: I remember seeing myself doing no more than rubbing it from the garment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"256","264 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, he said: Shubah informed us, from al-Hakam, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"257","265 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mabad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai, from Hammam, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it."
"258","266 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from Hammam, and he mentioned the like of it."
"259","267 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah narrated to us, from Zayd, from al-Amash, and he mentioned the like of it with its chain."
"260","268 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Hafs informed us, from al-Amash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad ibn Yazid and Hammam, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it."
"261","269 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Hammam, from Aishah, the like of it."
"262","270 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Masudi narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Hammam, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it, except that he said: I remember seeing myself doing no more than scraping it from the garment; and when it dried, I rubbed it."
"263","271 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma narrated to us, he said: Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: Wasil al-Ahdab narrated to us, from Ibrahim al-Nakhai, from al-Aswad, he said: Aishah saw me washing janabah from my garment, and said: I remember seeing myself, when it would touch the garment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), doing no more than doing like this with it - meaning rubbing it."
"264","272 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Duhaym narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, from Ata, from Aishah, she said: I used to rub it from the garment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - meaning mani."
"265","273 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Abu Hashim, from Abu Mijlaz, from al-Harith ibn Nawfal, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it."
"266","274 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Sari narrated to us, he said: Mubashshir ibn Ismail narrated to us, he said: Jafar ibn Burqan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I used to rub mani from the woolen cloak of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and our cloak in those days was wool."
"267","275 - Ahmad ibn Abdillah ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Barqi narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, from al-Awzai, from Yahya ibn Said, from Amrah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I used to rub mani from the garment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when it was dry, and wash it or wipe it when it was moist - al-Humaydi doubted."
"268","276 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abthar ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Burd, the brother of Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abu Saffanah al-Nakhai, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I used to rub mani from the garment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Jafar - Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Tahawi, may Allah have mercy on him - said: So some goers held that mani is pure, and that it does not spoil water even if it falls into it, and that its ruling in that is the ruling of phlegm; and they cited these reports as proof. Others disagreed with them, saying: Rather it is impure. They said: You have no proof in these reports, for they only came mentioning sleeping garments, and did not come concerning garments one prays in. We have seen garments impure with stool, urine, and blood: there is no harm in sleeping in them, but prayer in them is not permitted. So it is possible that mani is likewise. This hadith would only be proof against us if we said: sleeping in an impure garment is not sound; but since we permit that and agree with what you narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that, and further say: prayer in that is not sound, we have opposed nothing narrated on that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And there has come from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, concerning what she used to do with the garment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in which he used to pray when mani touched it."
"269","277 - What Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn al-Mubarak and Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal narrated to us, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I used to wash mani from the garment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he would go out to prayer while spots of water were still on his garment."
"270","278 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from Amr, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"271","279 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Amr informed us, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. Abu Jafar said: So thus would Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, do with the garment of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in which he used to pray: she would wash mani from it, and rub it from his garment in which he did not pray. And that agreed with what was narrated from Umm Habibah."
"272","280 - Rabi al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to me, from Jafar ibn Rabiah, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Suwayd ibn Qays, from Muawiyah ibn Hudayj, from Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan, that he asked his sister Umm Habibah, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Did the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pray in the garment in which he slept with you? She said: Yes, so long as no harm had touched it."
"273","281 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr, Ibn Lahiah, and al-Layth informed me, from Yazid, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. And there has been narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, also what agrees with that."
"274","282 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated to us, from Ashath, from Muhammad, from Abdillah ibn Shaqiq, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not pray in the quilts of his wives."
"275","283 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: Ghundar narrated to us, from Shubah, from Ashath, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it, except that he said: in our quilts. Abu Jafar said: So it is established by what we mentioned that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not pray in the garment in which he slept when anything of janabah had touched it; and it is established that what al-Aswad and Hammam mentioned from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerns only the sleeping garment, not the prayer garment."
"276","284 - So among the proof of the people of the first saying against the people of the second saying in that is what Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdillah informed us, from Khalid, from Abu Mashar, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah and al-Aswad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I used to rub mani from the garment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), dry, with my fingers; then he would pray in it and not wash it."
"277","285 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Said narrated to us, he said: Sharik informed us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Hammam, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it."
"278","286 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj and Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, they said: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: I used to rub it from the garment of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he would pray in it."
"279","287 - Rabi al-Muadhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Qazaah ibn Suwayd narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Araj and Abdillah ibn Abi Najih narrated to me, from Mujahid, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it."
"280","288 - Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Qasim ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, the like of it. They said: So in these reports she used to rub mani from the prayer garment as she rubbed it from the sleeping garment. Abu Jafar said: In our view there is no proof in this for its purity; for it is possible that she used to do this with it, so the garment becomes pure thereby while mani in itself is impure, as has been narrated concerning what touches the sandal of harm."
"281","289 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, he said: al-Awzai narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, from Said al-Maqburi, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When one of you steps upon harm with his khuff or his sandal, their purification is the soil. Abu Jafar said: So that soil sufficed instead of washing them, yet there is no proof in that for the purity of the harm in itself. Likewise what we narrated concerning mani: it is possible that its ruling with them was likewise - the garment becomes pure by their removing it from it by rubbing, while it in itself is impure, just as the sandal became pure by their removing the harm from it, while it in itself is impure. So what we have arrived at from these reports narrated concerning mani is that the garment becomes pure from what touched it of that by rubbing when it is dry, and that suffices instead of washing; and there is no proof in any of this for its own ruling, whether it is pure or impure. So one goer held that there has been narrated from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, what indicates that with her it was impure, and he mentioned in that."
"282","290 - What Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, from Shubah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim, from his father, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said concerning mani when it touches the garment: If you see it, wash it, and if you do not see it, sprinkle it."
"283","291 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"284","292 - Sulayman ibn Shuayb narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Hafs informed me, he said: I heard my paternal aunt narrating from Aishah the like of it."
"285","293 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Shubah narrated to us, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. He said: So this indicates its impurity with her. It is said to him: There is no proof in that for what you mentioned; for had its ruling with her been the ruling of the rest of the impurities of stool, urine, and blood, she would have commanded washing the whole garment when its spot upon it is unknown. Do you not see that a garment touched by urine whose place is hidden is not purified by sprinkling, and it must be washed entirely until its purity from impurity is known. So since the ruling of mani - with Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her - when its spot on the garment is unknown, was sprinkling, it is thereby established that its ruling with her differed from the rest of the impurities. And the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) differed on that, and there was narrated from them on that what follows."
"286","294 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Said narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Husayn informed us, from Musab ibn Sad, from his father, that he used to rub janabah from his garment. So this is possible that he used to do that because with him it was pure; and it is possible that he used to do that as is done with dung scraped from the sandal, not because with him it was pure."
"287","295 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib, that he performed umrah with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, in a company among whom was Amr ibn al-As; and that Umar camped at part of the road, near some water. Then Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, had a wet dream when morning had almost come, and found no water in the company; so he rode until he came to water, and began washing what he saw of the wet dream, until dawn broke. So Amr said to him: Morning has come, and we have garments with us, so leave your garment. Umar said: Rather I wash what I saw and sprinkle what I did not see."
"288","296 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Zubayd ibn al-Salt, that he said: I went out with Umar ibn al-Khattab to al-Jurf, and he looked, and behold he had had a wet dream and had not performed ghusl. So he said: By Allah, I deem naught but that I had a wet dream without feeling it, and I prayed without performing ghusl. So he performed ghusl, and washed what he saw on his garment and sprinkled what he did not see. As for what Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated from Umar, it indicates that Umar did what he necessarily had to do due to the narrowness of the prayer time, and none of those with him denied that of him, so that indicates their following him upon what he deemed of that. As for his saying: and I sprinkle what I did not see with water, that is possible that he meant by it: and I sprinkle what I did not see of what I imagine touched it, without being certain of that, so that it cuts off doubt from him in what follows, and he says: This wetness is from water."
"289","297 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Abdillah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Talhah ibn Abdillah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said - concerning mani touching the garment: If you see it, wash it, and if not, wash the whole garment. So this indicates that he deemed it impure."
"290","298 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Habib, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: Wipe with idhkhir. So this indicates that he deemed it pure."
"291","299 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Abdurrahman narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, similarly."
"292","300 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mis'ar, from Jabalah ibn Suhaym, he said: I asked Ibn Umar about semen that gets on a garment. He said: Sprinkle it with water. It is possible that by sprinkling he meant washing, for sprinkling is sometimes called washing. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I know a city whose side the sea sprinkles, meaning the sea strikes its side. And it is possible that Ibn Umar meant something else."
"293","301 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, he said: Jabir ibn Samurah was asked, while I was with him, about a man who prays in the garment in which he has intercourse with his wife. He said: Pray in it, unless you see something in it, then wash it and do not sprinkle it, for sprinkling only makes it worse."
"294","302 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: al-Walid narrated to us, he said: al-Sari ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim ibn Rashid, he said: Anas ibn Malik was asked about a velvet cloth (qatfah) affected by janabah whose spot is unknown. He said: Wash it. Abu Ja'far said: Since there is this disagreement about it, and there is no proof in what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as to its ruling, we considered it by reasoning. We found the emission of semen to be the gravest of ritual impurities (hadath), because it necessitates the greatest purification. So we looked at things whose emission is a hadath, what is their ruling in themselves? We saw excrement and urine: their emission is a hadath, and they are impure in themselves. Likewise menstrual blood and irregular bleeding: they are a hadath, and they are impure in themselves, and the blood of veins is likewise by reasoning. Since it is established by what we mentioned that everything whose emission is a hadath is impure in itself, and it is established that the emission of semen is a hadath, it is also established that it is impure in itself. This is the reasoning about it, except that we followed, regarding the permissibility of its ruling when it is dry, what was narrated about that from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah Almighty have mercy on them."
"295","Chapter 13: One who has intercourse but does not ejaculate. 303 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith narrated to us, he said: Ubayy narrated to us, he said: Husayn al-Mu'allim narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, that he asked Uthman ibn Affan about a man who has intercourse but does not ejaculate. He said: Nothing is due upon him except purification (ablution). Then he said: I heard it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: And I asked Ali ibn Abi Talib, al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, Talhah ibn Ubaydillah, and Ubayy ibn Ka'b, and they said the same."
"296","304 - He said: And Abu Salamah informed me, he said: Urwah narrated to me that he asked Abu Ayyub, and he said the same."
"297","305 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, and he mentioned the same with its chain, except that he did not mention Ali, nor the question of Urwah to Abu Ayyub."
"298","306 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Husayn al-Mu'allim, from Yahya, from Abu Salamah, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Zayd ibn Khalid, he said: I asked Uthman about a man who has intercourse with his wife then fails to ejaculate (yak sul). He said: No ghusl is due upon him. Then I went to al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam and Ubayy ibn Ka'b, and they said the same from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"299","307 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us. (Ha). 308 - And Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is nothing for failure to ejaculate (iksal) except purification (ablution)."
"300","309 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Abdah ibn Sulayman informed us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: Abu Ayyub al-Ansari narrated to me, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, he said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a man who has intercourse but fails to ejaculate. He said: He washes what has touched him and performs ablution as for prayer."
"301","310 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Urwah ibn Iyad, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: I said to my brothers among the Ansar: Accept the matter as you say, water is from water; what if he performs ghusl? They said: No, by Allah, so that there remains in your heart no unease about what Allah and His Messenger have decreed."
"302","311 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Dhakwan Abu Salih, from Abu Sa'id, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a man of the Ansar and called him. He came out to him with his head dripping water. He said: Perhaps we hurried you. He said: Yes. He said: If you are hurried or your water is withheld (i.e. lost), then ablution is due upon you."
"303","312 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: My uncle Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Ibn Shihab informed him, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Sa'id, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Water is from water."
"304","313 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Su'ad, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"305","314 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: al-Ala ibn Muhammad Sayyar narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent for a man of the Ansar and he delayed. He said: What kept you? He said: I had been with my wife, and when your messenger came I performed ghusl, though I had not done anything new. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Water is from water, and ghusl is upon whoever ejaculates. Abu Ja'far said: So a group held that whoever penetrates the private part but does not ejaculate owes no ghusl, and they adduced these reports. Others differed, saying: Ghusl is due upon him even if he does not ejaculate. And they adduced in that what follows."
"306","315 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj and Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, they said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim narrated to me, from his father, from Aishah, that she was asked about a man who has intercourse but does not ejaculate. She said: I and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did it, and we both performed ghusl from it."
"307","316 - Muhammad ibn Bahr ibn Matar al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us. (Ha). 317 - And Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Thabit, from Abdullah ibn Rabah, from Abd al-Aziz ibn al-Nu'man, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: When the two circumcisions met, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would perform ghusl."
"308","318 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, he said: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned: when the two circumcisions meet, does it make ghusl obligatory? Abu Musa said: I will bring you knowledge of that. He rose and I followed him until he came to Aishah. He said: O Mother of the Believers, I wish to ask you about something, but I am shy to ask you. She said: Ask, for I am only your mother. He said: When the two circumcisions meet, is ghusl obligatory? She said: When the two circumcisions met, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would perform ghusl."
"309","319 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"310","320 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Iyad ibn Abdullah al-Fihri and Ibn Lahi'ah informed me, from Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: Umm Kulthum informed me, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that a man asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about a man who has intercourse with his wife then fails to ejaculate: is ghusl due upon him? And Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, was sitting. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I do that, I and this one, then we perform ghusl. They said: So these reports tell of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he would perform ghusl when he had intercourse even without ejaculation. It was said to them: These reports tell only of the act of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he may do what is not obligatory upon him, while the first reports tell of what is obligatory and what is not, so they take precedence. Part of the proof of the holders of the second view against the first is that the reports we narrated in the first section of this chapter are of two kinds: one kind is only 'water is from water', and the other is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no ghusl upon one who fails to ejaculate until he ejaculates. As for what among that mentions 'water is from water', it has been narrated from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, that the intent of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by it was other than what the holders of the first view took it to mean."
"311","321 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Dawud, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, his saying: Water is from water applies only to wet dreams, when one sees that he has intercourse then does not ejaculate, so no ghusl is due upon him. So this Ibn Abbas has informed that its sense is other than the sense the holders of the first view gave it, and his saying contradicts theirs. As for what was narrated making the matter explicit, stating purposely that there is no ghusl in that until there is fluid, it has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the contrary."
"312","322 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Abu Rafi', from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When he sits between her four parts then exerts himself, ghusl is obligatory."
"313","323 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, he said: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hammam and Aban narrated to us, from Qatadah, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"314","324 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Abu Rafi', from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"315","325 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, from Aishah, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When he sits between her four parts then joins circumcision to circumcision, ghusl is obligatory."
"316","326 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: My uncle narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah, from Habban ibn Wasi', from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Aishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When circumcision passes circumcision, ghusl is obligatory. Abu Ja'far said: So these reports contradict the first reports, and there is in none of that evidence of which is the abrogating one. So we looked into that."
"317","327 - And Ali ibn Shaybah has narrated to us, he said: al-Himmani narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Zuhri, from Sahl ibn Sa'd, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, he said: Water is from water was only at the beginning of Islam; when Allah consolidated the matter, He forbade it."
"318","328 - Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: My uncle narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, he said: Ibn Shihab said: One whom I trust narrated to me, from Sahl ibn Sa'd al-Sa'idi, that Ubayy ibn Ka'b al-Ansari informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made water is from water a concession at the beginning of Islam, then forbade that and commanded ghusl."
"319","329 - Yazid ibn Sinan al-Fath and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Sahl ibn Sa'd al-Sa'idi said, he said: Ubayy ibn Ka'b narrated to me, then he mentioned the same. Abu Ja'far said: So this Ubayy informs that this is the abrogator of the saying: water is from water. And there has been narrated from him after that of his saying what also points to this."
"320","330 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id informed us, from Abdullah ibn Ka'b, from Mahmud ibn Labid, that he asked Zayd ibn Thabit about a man who has intercourse with his wife then fails to ejaculate and does not emit. Zayd said: He performs ghusl. I said to him: Ubayy ibn Ka'b used to hold there is no ghusl in it. Zayd said: Ubayy withdrew from that before he died."
"321","331 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, and he mentioned the same with its chain. Abu Ja'far said: So this Ubayy has said this, and there has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the contrary, so this is not possible in our view except that the abrogation of that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had been established with him."
"322","332 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, and Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), used to say: When circumcision touches circumcision, ghusl is obligatory. So this Uthman also says this, though the contrary has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); this is only possible if the abrogation had been established with him."
"323","333 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Sa'igh narrated to us, he said: Habib ibn Shihab narrated to us, from his father, he said: I asked Abu Hurayrah what makes ghusl obligatory. He said: When the rounded head disappears. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what we have mentioned from him in this chapter contradicting that; this too is proof of the abrogation of that."
"324","334 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Amr ibn Murrah al-Jamali, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, he said: Men of the Ansar used to rule that when a man has intercourse with a woman without ejaculating, no ghusl is due upon him, while the Muhajirun did not follow them in that. This indicates the abrogation of that too, for Uthman and al-Zubayr are of the Muhajirun, and they had heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what we narrated from them at the beginning of this chapter, then they said the contrary; that is only possible if the abrogation had been established with them. Then Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, disclosed that in the presence of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among Muhajirun and Ansar, and it was not confirmed with him, so he made the people adopt other than it and commanded them ghusl, and no one objected to him in that, and they accepted it from him; that is proof of their return to his view too."
"325","335 - Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Ma'mar ibn Abi Habibah, he said: I heard Ubayd ibn Rifa'ah al-Ansari say: We were in a gathering in which was Zayd ibn Thabit, and we discussed ghusl from ejaculation. Zayd said: None of you, when he has intercourse without ejaculating, owes more than washing his private part and performing ablution as for prayer. A man of the gathering rose and went to Umar and told him of that. Umar said to the man: Go yourself and bring him to me so that you are the witness against him. He went and brought him, while with Umar were men of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), among them Ali ibn Abi Talib and Mu'adh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with them. Umar said: Are you an enemy to yourself, ruling the people thus? Zayd said: By Allah, I did not invent it, but I heard it from my uncles Rifa'ah ibn Rafi' and Abu Ayyub al-Ansari. Umar said to those with him of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): What do you say? They differed before him. Umar said: O servants of Allah, whom shall I ask after you, when you are the best people of Badr? Ali ibn Abi Talib said to him: Send to the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for if there is anything of that, it will have appeared to them. He sent to Hafsah and asked her; she said: I have no knowledge of that. Then he sent to Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and she said: When circumcision passes circumcision, ghusl is obligatory. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said thereupon: I know of no one who does it then does not perform ghusl but I will make him an example."
"326","336 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, he said: Ibn Idris narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq. (Ha). 337 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ayyash ibn al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Abd al-A'la ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Ma'mar ibn Abi Habibah, from Ubayd ibn Rifa'ah, from his father, he said: I was sitting with Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, when a man came and said: O Commander of the Believers, this Zayd ibn Thabit rules the people about ghusl from janabah by his own opinion. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Bring him to me quickly. So Zayd came. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: It has reached me that you rule the people about ghusl from janabah by your opinion in the mosque of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Zayd said to him: By Allah, O Commander of the Believers, I did not rule by my opinion, but I heard something from my uncles and said it. He said: From which of your uncles? He said: From Ubayy ibn Ka'b, Abu Ayyub, and Rifa'ah ibn Rafi'. Umar turned to me and said: What does this youth say? He said: I said: We used to do it in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) then not perform ghusl. He said: Did you ask the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that? I said: No. He said: Bring the people to me. The people agreed that water is only from water, except Ali and Mu'adh ibn Jabal, who said: When circumcision passes circumcision, ghusl is obligatory. He said: O Commander of the Believers, I find no one more knowledgeable about the matter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than his wives. He sent to Hafsah, may Allah be pleased with her, and she said: I have no knowledge. He sent to Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and she said: When circumcision passes circumcision, ghusl is obligatory. Umar was crushed (moved) and said: If I am told of anyone who does it then does not perform ghusl, I will punish him severely."
"327","338 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Ma'mar ibn Abi Habibah narrated to me, from Ubaydullah ibn Adi ibn al-Khiyar, he said: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) discussed ghusl from janabah in the presence of Umar ibn al-Khattab. Some said: When circumcision passes circumcision, ghusl is obligatory. And some said: Water is only from water. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: You have differed before me while you are the best people of Badr; what of the people after you? Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O Commander of the Believers, if you wish to know that, send to the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and ask them. He sent to Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and she said: When circumcision passes circumcision, ghusl is obligatory. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said thereupon: I shall hear of no one saying water is from water but I will make him an example. So this Umar made the people adopt this in the presence of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and no denier denied it to him. And the saying of Rifa'ah in the hadith of Ibn Ishaq, that the people said water is from water, may mean Umar did not accept that, for it may be upon what they understood of it; and it may be as Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, said. When they did not establish it for him, he left their saying and went to what he and the rest of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) held. And there has been narrated from others among them what agrees with that too."
"328","339 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them, he said: The Muhajirun agreed that whatever makes the hadd of flogging and stoning obligatory upon him makes ghusl obligatory: Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman, and Ali, may Allah be pleased with them."
"329","340 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Abdullah, about a man who has intercourse but does not ejaculate. He said: When you reach that, perform ghusl."
"330","341 - Yazid narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, from Abdullah, the same."
"331","342 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: When circumcision meets circumcision, ghusl is obligatory."
"332","343 - Rawh narrated to us, he said: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from al-Saq'ab ibn Zuhayr, from Abdullah ibn al-Aswad, he said: My father used to send me to Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, before I reached puberty. When I reached puberty I came and called out and said: What makes ghusl obligatory? She said: When the two shaved parts meet."
"333","344 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Abu al-Nadr, from Abu Salamah, he said: I asked Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, what makes ghusl obligatory? She said: When circumcision passes circumcision, ghusl is obligatory."
"334","345 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim, from Maymun ibn Mihran, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: When the two circumcisions meet, ghusl is obligatory."
"335","346 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma narrated to us, he said: Juwayriyah narrated to us, from Nafi', from Abdullah, he said: When circumcision meets circumcision, ghusl is obligatory."
"336","347 - Ahmad narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, the same. Abu Ja'far said: So the soundness of the view of whoever holds ghusl obligatory by the meeting of the two circumcisions is established by these reports we narrated. This is the aspect of this chapter by way of reports. As for its aspect by way of reasoning: we saw they did not differ that intercourse in the private part without ejaculation is a hadath. One group said: it is the gravest of hadaths, so they made the gravest purification obligatory in it, namely ghusl. Another group said: it is like the lightest of hadaths, so they made the lightest purification obligatory in it, namely ablution. So we wanted to consider the meeting of the two circumcisions: is it the gravest of things, so we make the gravest of what is due obligatory in it? We found things that intercourse necessitates, namely spoiling of fasting and hajj, and that is by the meeting of the two circumcisions even without ejaculation; in hajj it necessitates blood, and making up the hajj, and in fasting it necessitates making up and expiation, in the view of whoever makes it obligatory. Had he had intercourse below the private part, only blood would be due in hajj, and nothing would be due in fasting unless he ejaculates, though all that is forbidden to him in his hajj and fasting. Whoever commits zina with a woman is subjected to hadd even without ejaculating; had he done it under doubt so the hadd falls, mahr is due. Had he had intercourse with her below the private part, neither hadd nor mahr would be due, but he is disciplined if there is no doubt. When a man marries a woman and has intercourse with her without seclusion in the private part then divorces her, mahr is due whether he ejaculated or not, iddah is due upon her, and that makes her lawful for her first husband. Had he had intercourse with her below the private part, nothing of that would be due, and in divorce half the mahr is due if he had named a mahr, or mut'ah if he had not named one. So in these things we described, without ejaculation, the gravest of what is due in intercourse with ejaculation is due, of hadd punishments, dowries, and other. So by reasoning it is likewise, in the ruling of hadaths, the gravest of hadaths, and the gravest of what is due in hadaths is due in it, namely ghusl. And another proof in that: we saw these things made obligatory by the meeting of the two circumcisions, such that if ejaculation follows, no second ruling is due by the ejaculation; the ruling belongs to the meeting of the two circumcisions. Do you not see that had a man had unlawful intercourse with a woman and the two circumcisions met, hadd is due upon them thereby, and had he remained upon her until he ejaculated, no punishment beyond the hadd due by the meeting would be due; and had that intercourse been under doubt so mahr became due by the meeting, then he remained until he ejaculated, nothing would be due in that ejaculation after what became due by the meeting. And what is ruled in these things for one who had intercourse and ejaculated is what is ruled for him when he had intercourse without ejaculating, and the ruling in that belongs to the meeting of the two circumcisions, not to the ejaculation after it. So by reasoning, the ghusl due upon one who had intercourse and ejaculated is by the meeting of the two circumcisions, not by the ejaculation after it. Thus the saying of those who said intercourse makes ghusl obligatory, with or without ejaculation, is established. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, Muhammad, and the generality of scholars, may Allah Almighty have mercy on them. And another proof in that: that Fahd..."
"337","348 - He narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah narrated to us, from Zayd, from Jabir, that is Ibn Yazid, from Abu Salih, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, preaching. He said: The women of the Ansar rule that when a man has intercourse without ejaculating, ghusl is due upon the woman but no ghusl upon him, but it is not as they ruled; when circumcision passes circumcision, ghusl is obligatory. Abu Ja'far said: In this report is that the Ansar held that water is from water applies only to the men who have intercourse, not to the women, and that mere contact makes ghusl obligatory upon women even without ejaculation. We have seen ejaculation, the ruling of women and men is equal in it, in the obligatoriness of ghusl upon them. So by reasoning, the ruling of contact without ejaculation should be equal for men and women, in the obligatoriness of ghusl upon them."
"338","Chapter 14: Eating what fire has altered, does it make ablution obligatory or not? 349 - Ibn Abi Dawud and Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Matar al-Warraq, he said: I said: From whom did al-Hasan take ablution from what fire has altered? He said: Al-Hasan took it from Anas, Anas took it from Abu Talhah, and Abu Talhah took it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"339","350 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Zuhri narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to me, from his father, that is Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Abd al-Qari, from Abu Talhah, Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he ate a piece (thawr) of dried cheese (aqit) and performed ablution from it. Amr said: Al-thawr is a piece."
"340","351 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr, from Kharijah ibn Zayd, from Zayd ibn Thabit, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: Perform ablution from what fire has altered."
"341","352 - Ibn Abi Dawud and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn Musafir narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"342","353 - Nasr ibn Marzuq and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"343","354 - Fahd and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth informed me, he said: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Sa'id ibn Khalid ibn Amr ibn Uthman informed me that he asked Urwah ibn al-Zubayr about that. Urwah said: I heard Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, and he mentioned the same."
"344","355 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Harb ibn Shaddad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, he said: Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf narrated to me that Abu Sufyan ibn Sa'id ibn al-Mughirah informed him that he entered upon Umm Habibah, may Allah be pleased with her, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and she called for sawiq for him and he drank, then she said: O son of my brother, perform ablution. He said: I have not innovated anything. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perform ablution from what fire has touched."
"345","356 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah, from Bakr ibn Sawadah, from Muhammad ibn Muslim ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Sufyan ibn Sa'id ibn al-Akhnas, from Umm Habibah, may Allah be pleased with her, the same, except that he said: O son of my sister."
"346","357 - Ibn Abi Dawud and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"347","358 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perform ablution from what fire has altered, even from a piece of dried cheese."
"348","359 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perform ablution from a piece of dried cheese."
"349","360 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Muqaddami narrated to us, he said: Abd al-A'la narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Perform ablution from what fire has touched, even from a piece of dried cheese. Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O Abu Hurayrah, we anoint with oil heated by fire, and we perform ablution with water heated by fire. He said: O son of my brother, when you hear a hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), do not strike examples against it."
"350","361 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to us, he said: al-Harith ibn Ya'qub narrated to us that Irak ibn Malik informed him, he said: I heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Perform ablution from what fire has touched."
"351","362 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Ishaq ibn Bakr narrated to me, from my father, from Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah, from Bakr ibn Sawadah, from Muhammad ibn Muslim, from Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Qariz, he said: I saw Abu Hurayrah performing ablution on the back of the mosque. He said: I ate pieces of dried cheese and performed ablution; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Perform ablution from what fire has touched."
"352","363 - Fahd and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to me, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"353","364 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr al-Awza'i, from al-Muttalib ibn Hantab, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"354","364 (m) - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'mar narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Husayn al-Mu'allim, from Yahya, and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"355","365 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, from Mu'awiyah ibn Salih, from Sulayman ibn Abi al-Rabi', from al-Qasim, freedman of Mu'awiyah, he said: I came to the mosque and saw the people gathered around a shaykh narrating to them. I said: Who is this? They said: Sahl ibn al-Hanzaliyyah. I heard him say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever eats meat, let him perform ablution."
"356","366 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from a man of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: We used to perform ablution from what fire has altered, rinse from milk, and not rinse from dates. So a group held ablution from what fire has altered, adducing these reports. Others differed, saying: There is no ablution in any of that. 367 - And they held in that what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that."
"357","368 - Yunus narrated to me, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him. (Ha). And Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Malik narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Ibn Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate a shoulder of sheep then prayed without performing ablution."
"358","369 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, and he mentioned the like with its chain."
"359","370 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Zubayr al-Hanzali informed us, from Ali ibn Abdullah ibn al-Abbas, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like."
"360","371 - Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Suri narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, he said: Ibn Thawban narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Ali, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"361","372 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Yahya ibn Ya'mar, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"362","373 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from Abu Nu'aym, that is Wahb ibn Kaysan, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate bread and meat, then he mentioned the same."
"363","374 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Halhalah al-Duwali, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Ata, that he entered upon Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, one day in the house of Maymunah. He struck my hand and said: I am amazed at people who perform ablution from what fire has touched; by Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) once gathered his garments upon him, then tharid was brought and he ate from it, then he rose and went out to prayer without performing ablution."
"364","375 - Yunus and al-Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, they said: Asad narrated to us. (Ha). 376 - And Bakr ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us. (Ha)."
"365","377 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: I heard Abu Awn Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Thaqafi say: I heard Abdullah ibn Shaddad ibn al-Had narrate from Umm Salamah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to prayer, and a shoulder was picked out for him and he ate from it, then he went out and prayed without performing ablution."
"366","378 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Abu Awn, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Shaddad say: Marwan asked Abu Hurayrah about ablution from what fire has altered, and he commanded him with it. Then he said: How can we ask anyone when among us are the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? They sent to Umm Salamah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and asked her, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Shu'bah."
"367","379 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Muhammad ibn Yusuf, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, from Umm Salamah, she said: I brought near to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a roasted side, and he ate from it without performing ablution."
"368","380 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Za'idah ibn Qudamah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil narrated to us, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: Food was brought to us while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was with us; we ate then rose to prayer and none of us performed ablution. Then we had supper from the rest of the sheep, then rose to Asr prayer, and none of us touched water."
"369","381 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ... and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"370","382 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: A woman of the Ansar invited us and slaughtered a sheep for us ... and he mentioned the hadith; she sprinkled a wall (?) for us, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for purification water. We ate then he prayed without performing ablution."
"371","383 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Umarah ibn Zadham narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, he said: I entered upon one of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Narrate to me about something fire has altered. She said: Seldom would the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) come to us but we would fry for him a grain that is in Madinah, and he would eat from it and pray without performing ablution."
"372","384 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Umarah ibn Zadham narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, he said: I entered upon so-and-so, one of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - he had named her and I forgot. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me while a sheep's belly hung with me. He said: If only you would cook for us from this belly such and such. She said: So we made it and he ate without performing ablution."
"373","385 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ammar ibn Abi Ammar, from Umm Hakim, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me and ate a shoulder; Bilal called him with the adhan, and he prayed without performing ablution."
"374","386 (a, b) - Ibn Marzuq, Rabi' al-Jizi, and Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, they said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Fa'id, freedman of Ubaydullah ibn Ali, narrated to us, from Ubaydullah, from his grandfather, he said: A sheep's belly was cooked for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he ate from it, then prayed Isha without performing ablution."
"375","387 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, from al-Mughirah ibn Abi Rafi', from Abu Rafi', from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like, though he did not mention Isha."
"376","388 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Salim narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abi Humayd, he said: Hind bint Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Khudri narrated to me, from her paternal aunt, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) visited us then ate with us a sheep's shoulder, then rose and prayed without performing ablution."
"377","389 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Nasr ibn Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Ziyad, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith al-Zubaydi, he said: We ate with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) food in the mosque that had been roasted; then prayer was established, so we wiped our hands with pebbles, then rose and prayed without performing ablution."
"378","390 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdullah al-Uwaysi narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, he said: Ja'far ibn Amr ibn Umayyah informed me that his father said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) eat a foreleg, cutting from it; he was called to prayer, so he rose, threw down the knife, and prayed without performing ablution."
"379","391 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Bushayr ibn Yasar, freedman of Banu Harithah, that Suwayd ibn al-Nu'man narrated to him that he went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of Khaybar, until when they were at al-Sahba, which is of the nearest part of Khaybar, he alighted and prayed Asr, then called for provisions, and nothing but sawiq was brought. He ordered it moistened, and he ate and we ate. Then he rose to Maghrib, rinsed and we rinsed, then prayed without performing ablution."
"380","392 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Yahya, and he mentioned the like with its chain, except that he did not say: which is of the nearest part of Khaybar."
"381","393 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: al-Ju'ayd ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from al-Hasan ibn Abdullah ibn Ubaydillah, that Amr ibn Ubaydillah narrated to him, he said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) eat a shoulder then rise and pray without performing ablution."
"382","394 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Isma'il narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Thabit and others of the elders of Banu Abd al-Ashhal, from Umm Amir bint Yazid, a woman of those who pledged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that she came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a bone with meat in the mosque of Banu Abd al-Ashhal; he stripped it, then rose and prayed without performing ablution. In these reports is what negates eating what fire has touched being a hadath, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not perform ablution from it. It may be that what he commanded of ablution in the first reports is the ablution of prayer, and it may be washing the hand, not the ablution of prayer; yet it is established from him by what we narrated that he performed ablution and that he did not perform ablution."
"383","395 - 396 - 397 - So we wanted to know which of that is later. And Ibn Abi Dawud, Abu Umayyah, and Abu Zur'ah al-Dimashqi narrated to us, they said: 397 - Ali ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, he said: The later of the two matters from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was leaving ablution from what fire has touched."
"384","398 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate a piece of dried cheese and performed ablution, then ate after it a shoulder and prayed without performing ablution. So it is established by what we mentioned that the later of the two matters from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is leaving ablution from what fire has altered, and what contradicts that is abrogated by the second act. This if what he commanded of ablution means the ablution of prayer. If it does not mean the ablution of prayer, then the first hadith does not establish eating what fire has altered as a hadath. So it is established by what we mentioned, by verifying these reports, that eating what fire has touched is not a hadath. And a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) also narrated that."
"385","399 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Rabah ibn Abi Ma'ruf narrated to us, from Ata, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him. (Ha)."
"386","400 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him. (Ha)."
"387","401 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Sulayman ibn Qays, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him. (Ha)."
"388","402 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him. (Ha)."
"389","403 - And Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him. (Ha)."
"390","404 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Za'idah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil narrated to us, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: We ate with Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, bread and meat, then he prayed without performing ablution. And in the hadith of Abdullah ibn Muhammad especially: And we ate with Umar bread and meat, then he rose to prayer without touching water."
"391","405 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Muhammad al-Munkadir, from Jabir, from Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them, the same."
"392","406 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Abu Nu'aym Wahb ibn Kaysan, that he heard Jabir ibn Abdullah say: I saw Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, eat meat then pray without performing ablution."
"393","407 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Hisham said to me: This one does not leave us, meaning al-Zuhri, but commands us to perform ablution from anything we eat. I said: I asked Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab about it and he said: When you eat it, it is good, no ablution is due upon you for it; when it comes out, it is foul, ablution is due upon you for it. He said: I see you two have differed; is there anyone in the town? I said: Yes, the oldest man in the Arabian Peninsula. He said: Who is he? I said: Ata. He sent, and he was brought. He said: These two have differed before me, what do you say? 408 - He said: Jabir ibn Abdullah narrated to us, then he mentioned from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, the same."
"394","409 - Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Maymun narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Ata, he said: Jabir narrated to me that he saw Abu Bakr do that."
"395","410 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Hammad, Mansur, Sulayman, and Mughirah, from Ibrahim, that Ibn Mas'ud and Alqamah left the house of Abdullah ibn Mas'ud heading for prayer; a bowl was brought from the house of Alqamah containing tharid and meat. They ate, then Ibn Mas'ud rinsed and washed his fingers, then rose to prayer."
"396","411 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: To perform ablution from a foul word is dearer to me than to perform ablution from a good morsel."
"397","412 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir and Safwan ibn Sulaym, that they informed him, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, from Rabi'ah ibn Abdullah ibn al-Hudayr, that he had supper with Umar ibn al-Khattab, then prayed without performing ablution."
"398","413 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Damrah ibn Sa'id al-Mazini, from Aban ibn Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, that Uthman ate bread and meat, washed his hands, wiped his face with them, then prayed without performing ablution."
"399","414 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Uways narrated to me, from Sulayman, from Utbah ibn Muslim, from Ubayd ibn Hunayn, he said: I saw Uthman brought tharid; he ate, then rinsed, washed his hand, then rose and led the people in prayer without performing ablution."
"400","415 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Nawfal ibn Abi Aqrab al-Kinani, he said: I saw Ibn Abbas eat thin bread and meat until grease ran upon his fingers; he washed his hand and prayed Maghrib."
"401","416 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, he said: Isra'il narrated to us, from Tariq, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, that Ibn Abbas was brought a large bowl of tharid and meat at Asr; he ate from it, then water was brought and he washed his fingertips, then prayed without performing ablution."
"402","417 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Raja narrated to us, he said: Za'idah informed us, from Abu Ishaq al-Sabi'i, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: People entered upon Ibn Abbas and he fed them food, then he led them in prayer upon a carpet; they placed their faces and foreheads upon it, and did not perform ablution."
"403","418 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Burdah, from his father, he said: Ibn Umar said to Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him: What do you say about ablution from what fire has altered? He said: Perform ablution from it. He said: What do you say about oil and heated water, is ablution performed from it? He said: You are a man of Quraysh, and I am a man of Daws. He said: O Abu Hurayrah, perhaps you take refuge in this verse: Nay, they are a contentious people."
"404","419 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Husayn, from Mujahid, he said: Ibn Umar said: Do not perform ablution from anything you eat."
"405","420 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Abu Ghalib, from Abu Umamah, that he ate bread and meat, prayed without performing ablution, and said: Ablution is from what comes out, not from what enters. Abu Ja'far said: So these eminent Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not hold ablution in eating what fire has altered. And there has been narrated from others among them the like, of those from whom it was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he commanded ablution from what fire has altered."
"406","421 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to us, he said: Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi narrated to me, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd al-Ansari narrated to me, he said: Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, he said: While I, Abu Talhah al-Ansari, and Ubayy ibn Ka'b were brought heated food and ate, I rose to prayer and performed ablution. One of them said to his companion: Is it an Iraqi practice? Then they rebuked me, and I knew they were more learned than I."
"407","422 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd al-Ansari, that Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, came from Iraq ... then he mentioned the like, adding: Abu Talhah and Ubayy rose and prayed without performing ablution."
"408","423 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, he said: Isma'il ibn Rafi' and Muhammad ibn al-Nil narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd al-Ansari, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I, Abu Talhah, and Abu Ayyub al-Ansari ate food touched by fire; I rose to perform ablution, and they said to me: Do you perform ablution from good things? You have brought an Iraqi practice. So this Abu Talhah and Abu Ayyub prayed after eating what fire had altered without performing ablution, though they had narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he commanded ablution from that, in what we narrated from them in this chapter. This, in our view, is only possible if the abrogation of what they narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had been established with them. This is the aspect of this chapter by way of reports. As for its aspect by way of reasoning: we have seen these things about whose eating there is disagreement whether it breaks ablution when fire touches them, while it is agreed that eating them before fire touches them does not break ablution. So we wanted to consider: does fire have a ruling that applies to things when it touches them so their ruling transfers? We saw pure water with which obligations are performed; then we saw it when heated, becoming of what fire has touched, that its ruling in purity remains as before fire touched it, and fire did not introduce a ruling transferring it from what it was at first. Since what we described is so, by reasoning pure food whose eating before fire touches it is not a hadath, when fire touches it, is not moved from its state nor its ruling changed; its ruling after fire touches it is as before, by analogy and reasoning as we explained. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah Almighty have mercy on them. And a group distinguished between sheep meat and camel meat, making ablution obligatory in eating camel meat but not in eating sheep meat."
"409","424 - And they adduced in that what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Simak narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Abi Thawr, from Jabir ibn Samurah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: Shall we perform ablution from camel meat? He said: Yes. It was said: Shall we perform ablution from sheep meat? He said: No."
"410","425 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, he said: Za'idah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ja'far ibn Abi Thawr, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like."
"411","426 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ja'far, from his grandfather Jabir ibn Samurah, that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I perform ablution from sheep meat? He said: If you wish do, and if you wish do not. He said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I perform ablution from camel meat? He said: Yes."
"412","427 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Abdullah ibn Mawhab, from Ja'far ibn Abi Thawr, from Jabir ibn Samurah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. Others differed, saying: No ablution for prayer is due by eating any of that. Part of their proof is that the ablution the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant may be washing the hand. One group distinguished between camel meat and sheep meat, because of the coarseness in camel meat and the predominance of its grease upon the eater's hand, so he did not permit leaving it upon the hand, while he permitted no ablution from sheep meat for lack of that. We narrated in the first chapter in the hadith of Jabir that the later of the two matters from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was leaving ablution from what fire has altered. If what preceded of it was ablution from what fire has touched, including camel meat and other, then his leaving that is leaving ablution from camel meat. This is the ruling of this chapter by way of reports. As for by way of reasoning: we have seen camels and sheep equal in the lawfulness of selling them, drinking their milk, purity of their meat, and that their rulings do not differ in any of that. So by reasoning they are equal in eating their meat. Just as there is no ablution in eating sheep meat, so there is no ablution in eating camel meat. This is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah Almighty have mercy on them."
"413","Chapter 15: Touching the private part, is ablution obligatory in it or not? 428 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: al-Husayn ibn Mahdi narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, he said: Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, that he and Marwan discussed ablution from touching the private part. Marwan said: Busrah bint Safwan narrated to me that she heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) command ablution from touching the private part. Urwah did not give her hadith much weight. Marwan sent a policeman to her; he returned and told them she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) command ablution from touching the private part. So a group held to this report and made ablution from touching the private part obligatory. Others differed, saying: There is no ablution in it; and they argued against the first group, saying: In this hadith of yours Urwah did not give the hadith of Busrah much weight. If that is because she is in his view in the state of one from whom that is not taken, then in the weakening of one lesser than Urwah, Busrah, is what drops her hadith; and others followed him in that."
"414","429 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Zayd informed me, from Rabi'ah, that he said: Had I placed my hand in blood or a menstruation cloth, my ablution would not be broken; so is touching the male organ lighter, or blood, or menstruation? He said: Rabi'ah used to say to them: Woe to you, would anyone adopt the like of this, while we act by the hadith of Busrah? By Allah, had Busrah testified about this sandal I would not accept her testimony; religion stands upon prayer, and prayer stands upon purification. Was there among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) none to uphold this religion but Busrah? Ibn Zayd said: Upon this we found our elders; not one of them held ablution in touching the male organ. If he only refrained from giving it weight because Marwan in his view is not in the state of one from whom acceptance is due, then the report of Marwan's policeman from Busrah is below his own report from her. If Marwan's own report in itself, in Urwah's view, is not accepted, then his policeman's report to him from her even more so is not accepted. This hadith too al-Zuhri did not hear from Urwah; he only glossed it over."
"415","430 - That is because Yunus narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Shihab, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Marwan ibn al-Hakam, he said: Ablution from touching the male organ. Marwan said: Busrah bint Safwan informed me of it. He sent to Busrah, and she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned what ablution is performed from, and mentioned touching the male organ. Abu Ja'far said: So this report is only from al-Zuhri, from Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, from Urwah. That lowers it a degree, for Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr's hadith from Urwah is not like al-Zuhri's hadith from Urwah, nor is Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr in their view precise in hadith. Indeed:"
"416","431 - Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to me, he said: Ibn Wazir narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy on him, say: I heard Ibn Uyaynah say: When we saw a man writing hadith with one of a group he named, among them Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, we laughed at him, for they did not know hadith. And you weaken what is like this with less than the speech of the like of Ibn Uyaynah. And others said: What is between al-Zuhri and Urwah in this hadith is Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad."
"417","432 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i narrated to me, he said: Ibn Shihab informed me, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm narrated to me, he said: Urwah narrated to me, from Busrah bint Safwan, that she heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: A man performs ablution from touching the male organ. If they say: Hisham ibn Urwah also narrated this hadith from his father, and Hisham is not of those whose narration is impugned at all, then they mentioned in that what follows."
"418","433 - Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, he said: Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: Marwan asked me about touching the male organ, and I said: There is no ablution in it. Marwan said: In it is ablution. Then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Abu Bakrah at the beginning of this chapter from Husayn ibn Mahdi."
"419","434 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Hisham ... and he mentioned the same with its chain, except that he said: So Urwah denied that."
"420","435 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, he said: Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from Hisham ... and he mentioned the same with its chain."
"421","436 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jumahi narrated to me, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Busrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: When one of you touches his male organ, let him not pray until he performs ablution."
"422","437 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from Hisham, from his father, from Marwan, from Busrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. It was said to him: Hisham ibn Urwah also did not hear this from his father; he took it from Abu Bakr, then glossed it as from his father."
"423","438 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Hazm narrated to me, from Urwah, that he was sitting with Marwan, then he mentioned the hadith as Ibn Abi Imran and Ibn Khuzaymah mentioned it; so the hadith returns to Abu Bakr too. 439 - If they say: Others besides al-Zuhri and Hisham also narrated it from Urwah, then they mentioned in that what follows."
"424","439 (a) - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj and Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, they said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Aswad narrated to us that he heard Urwah mention from Busrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same. It was said to them: How do you adduce Ibn Lahi'ah in this, when you do not make him a proof for your opponent in what he adduces against you? I did not intend by any of that disparagement of Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, nor of Ibn Lahi'ah, nor of others, but I intended to show the injustice of the opponent. So the weakness of al-Zuhri's hadith is established by what came between him and Urwah, and the weakness of al-Zuhri's and Hisham's hadith too by what is between Urwah and Busrah, for Urwah did not accept it nor give it weight; and a hadith has fallen with less than this. And if they adduce in that what follows."
"425","440 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, that he heard a man narrate in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), from Urwah, from Aishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with that. It was said to them: Your injustice suffices you, that you adduce the like of this. And if they adduce in that what follows."
"426","441 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, he said: My father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, he said: Muhammad ibn Muslim ibn Ubaydillah ibn Abdullah ibn Shihab narrated to me, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Zayd ibn Khalid, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever touches his private part, let him perform ablution."
"427","442 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ayyash al-Raqqam narrated to us, he said: Abd al-A'la narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq ... and he mentioned the same with its chain. It was said to him: You do not make Muhammad ibn Ishaq a proof in anything, when one like those who opposed him in this hadith opposes him, nor when he is alone. And this hadith itself is rejected (munkar), and likely to be a mistake, for Urwah, when Marwan asked him about touching the private part, answered from his opinion that there is no ablution in it. When Marwan told him from Busrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what he told, Urwah said to him: I have not heard of it. This was after the death of Zayd ibn Khalid by as much as Allah wills; how can it be that Urwah denies from Busrah what Zayd ibn Khalid had narrated to him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)?"
"428","443 - If he adduces in that what Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Isma'il ibn Abi Habibah al-Ashhali narrated to us, from Amr ibn Surayj, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with that."
"429","444 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Farwi Ishaq ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim narrated to us ... and he mentioned the same with its chain. It was said to them: You do not allow your opponent to adduce against you the like of Amr ibn Surayj, so how do you adduce him against him? Then that too in itself is rejected, for when Marwan told Urwah from Busrah what he told him of that, he had not known it before, neither from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, nor from other than her."
"430","445 - If they adduce in that what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Duhaym ibn al-Yatim narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us, from Sadaqah ibn Abdullah, from Hisham ibn Zayd, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with that. It was said to them: This Sadaqah ibn Abdullah is in your view weak; how do you adduce him? And Hisham ibn Zayd is not of the people of knowledge by whose narration the like of this is established. And if they adduce in that what follows."
"431","446 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, he said: al-Ala ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Whoever touches his private part, let him perform ablution. It was said to them: How do you adduce this al-Ala, when he is in your view weak? And if they adduce in that also what follows."
"432","447 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ma'n ibn Isa al-Qazzaz narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abd al-Malik, from al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah said: Whoever passes his hand to his male organ, with nothing between them, neither cover nor veil, let him perform ablution. It was said to them: This Yazid is in your view rejected in hadith, his hadith is not worth anything; how do you adduce him?"
"433","448 - And if they adduce in that what Yazid narrated to us, he said: Duhaym narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Nafi' al-Sa'igh narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from Uqbah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Thawban, from Jabir ibn Abdullah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of the hadith of Yunus from Ma'n. It was said to them: This hadith, all the precise narrators who narrated it from Ibn Abi Dhi'b sever it and stop it upon Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman."
"434","449 - Of that is what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from Uqbah, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with that. So these precise narrators stop this hadith upon Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman and differ in it with Ibn Nafi'; he is in your view a proof against him, while he is not a proof against them. How do you adduce a disconnected hadith in this, when you do not establish the disconnected?"
"435","450 - And if they adduce in that what Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman, Yunus, and Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, they said: Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, from al-Haytham ibn Humayd, he said: al-Ala ibn al-Harith informed me, from Makhul, from Anbasah ibn Abi Sufyan, from Umm Habibah, may Allah be pleased with her, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), she said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever touches his private part, let him perform ablution."
"436","451 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Mushir narrated to us, from al-Haytham... then he mentioned the same with its isnad. It was said to them: this hadith is also interrupted (munqati'), because Makhul did not hear anything from 'Anbasah ibn Abi Sufyan. 452 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated that to us, he said: I heard Abu Mushir say that, and you yourselves use Abu Mushir's statement as proof in cases like this."
"437","453 - And if they argue in this matter with what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ma'n ibn 'Isa narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Mu'ammal al-Makhzumi, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather: that Busrah asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: a woman strikes with her hand and touches her private part? He said: She should perform wudu, O Busrah."
"438","454 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Khattab ibn 'Uthman al-Fawzi narrated to us, he said: Baqiyyah narrated to us, from al-Zubaydi, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any man who touches his private part, let him perform wudu, and any woman who touches her private part, let her perform wudu. It was said to them: you claim that 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb did not hear anything from his father, and that his hadith from him is only from a written sheet, so this - by your own view - is interrupted, and the interrupted does not establish proof in your view. Thus the corruption of all these reports used as proof by those who require wudu for touching the private part is established. And reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) have been narrated contradicting that."
"439","455 - Among them is what Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Jabir, from Qays ibn Talq, from his father, that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): is there wudu for touching the penis? He said: No."
"440","456 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Musaddad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us... then he mentioned similar with its isnad."
"441","457 - Muhammad ibn al-'Abbas al-Lu'lu'i narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn 'Utbah narrated to us. (H). 458 - And Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Ayyub ibn 'Utbah narrated to us, from Qays ibn Talq, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar."
"442","459 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: Mulazim ibn 'Amr narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Badr al-Suhaymi, from Qays ibn Talq, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"443","460 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: al-Aswad ibn 'Amir, Khalaf ibn al-Walid, Ahmad ibn Yunus, and Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Qays, that he narrated to him from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar."
"444","461 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Mulazim narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Badr, from Qays ibn Talq, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that a man asked him: O Prophet of Allah, what do you think about a man touching his penis after performing wudu? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is it anything but a piece of flesh from you? Or: a morsel of flesh from you. This is Mulazim's hadith, sound (sahih) with a straight isnad, not confused in its isnad nor its text, so it is worthier - in our view - than what we first narrated of the reports confused in their isnads. 462 - And Ibn Abi 'Imran narrated to me, he said: I heard 'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-'Azim al-'Anbari say: I heard 'Ali ibn al-Madini say: this hadith of Mulazim is better than the hadith of Busrah. If this chapter is taken by way of the isnad and its soundness, then this hadith of Mulazim has the better isnad. And if it is taken by way of reasoning, then we see them unanimous that whoever touches his penis with the back of his hand or his forearms, no wudu is due for that. By reasoning, touching it with the palm should be the same. And we see that had he touched it with his thigh, no wudu would be due, though the thigh is private ('awrah). So if contact with it by the 'awrah does not require wudu, contact with it by other than the 'awrah is even less likely to require wudu. Those who required wudu for it said: the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) required wudu for contact with it by the palm."
"445","463 - They cited in that what Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: al-Hakam informed me, he said: I heard Mus'ab ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas say: I was holding the mushaf for my father and I touched my private part, so he ordered me to perform wudu."
"446","464 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, he said: Ibn 'Umar and Ibn 'Abbas used to say about a man touching his penis: they said, he performs wudu. Shu'bah said: I said to Qatadah: from whom is this? He said: from 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah."
"447","465 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, that he saw him pray a prayer he did not usually pray. He said: I said to him: what is this prayer? He said: I touched my private part and forgot to perform wudu."
"448","466 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, the same."
"449","467 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, from Mujahid, he said: we prayed with Ibn 'Umar - or: Ibn 'Umar led us in prayer - then he traveled, then he made his camel kneel. I said: O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, we have already prayed. He said: Abu 'Abd al-Rahman knows that, but I touched my penis. He said: so he performed wudu and repeated the prayer. It was said to them: as for what you narrated from Mus'ab ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, contrary to what al-Hakam narrated from him has been narrated from Mus'ab ibn Sa'd from his father."
"450","468 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Muhammad, from Mus'ab ibn Sa'd, he said: I was reciting the mushaf to my father, then I scratched myself and touched my private part. He said: did you touch your private part? I said: yes, I was scratching. He said: dip your hand in dust. And he did not order me to perform wudu. And it was narrated from Mus'ab also that his father ordered him to wash his hand."
"451","469 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us, he said: and Za'idah narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Zubayr ibn 'Adi, from Mus'ab ibn Sa'd, the same, except that he said: get up and wash your hand. So it is possible that the wudu which al-Hakam narrated in his hadith from Mus'ab is washing the hand, as al-Zubayr ibn 'Adi clarified from him, so that the two narrations do not contradict. And it was narrated from Sa'd, as his own statement: that there is no wudu in that."
"452","470 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us, he said: Za'idah informed us, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: Sa'd was asked about touching the penis, and he said: if it is impure then cut it off; there is no harm in it."
"453","471 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym informed us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, he said: a man said to Sa'd: he touched his penis while in prayer. He said: cut it off; it is only a piece of flesh from you. So this is Sa'd: when the narrations from him were examined, it was established from him that there is no wudu for touching the penis. As for what was narrated from Ibn 'Abbas requiring wudu for it, contrary to that has been narrated from him."
"454","472 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ishaq narrated to us, he said: 'Ikrimah ibn 'Ammar narrated to us, he said: 'Ata' narrated to us, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: I do not care whether I touched it or my nose."
"455","473 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from Shu'bah, the freedman of Ibn 'Abbas, from Ibn 'Abbas, the same."
"456","474 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash informed us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, that he did not consider touching the penis to require wudu. So this is Ibn 'Abbas: other than what Qatadah narrated from 'Ata' from him has been narrated from him. We do not know any Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who gave fatwa requiring wudu for it, except Ibn 'Umar. And most of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) opposed him in that."
"457","475 - Muhammad ibn al-'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, he said: Mis'ar informed us, from Qabus, from Abu Zibyan, from 'Ali, that he said: I do not care whether I touched my nose, my ear, or my penis."
"458","476 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Sulayman, from al-Minhal ibn 'Amr, from Qays ibn al-Sakan, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud said: I do not care whether I touched my penis in prayer, or my ear, or my nose."
"459","477 - Bakr ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: Abu Qays narrated to us, he said: I heard Huzayl narrate from ... 'Abdullah, similar."
"460","478 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash informed us, from al-Minhal ibn 'Amr, from Qays ibn al-Sakan, from 'Abdullah, the same."
"461","479 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Sulayman al-Shaybani informed us, from Abu Qays... then he mentioned the same with its isnad."
"462","480 - Abu Bakrah informed us, he said: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: Mis'ar narrated to us, from 'Umayr ibn Sa'id. (H). 481 - And Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Mis'ar narrated to us, from 'Umayr ibn Sa'id, he said: I was in a gathering in which 'Ammar ibn Yasir was present, and touching the penis was mentioned, so he said: it is only a piece of flesh from you, like my nose or your nose. And your palm has another place besides it."
"463","482 - Abu Bakrah informed us, he said: Abu 'Amir narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Iyad ibn Laqit, from al-Bara' ibn Qays. (H). 483 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mansur, he said: I heard a Sadusi narrate from al-Bara' ibn Qays. (H). 484 - And Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn Iyad ibn Laqit narrated to us, from his father, from al-Bara' ibn Qays, he said: I heard Hudhayfah say: I do not care whether I touched it or my nose."
"464","485 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us. (H). 486 - And Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Mukhariq ibn Ahmad, from Hudhayfah, similar."
"465","487 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Abi Razin narrated to us, he said: Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from al-Hasan, from five of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - among them 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, 'Imran ibn Husayn, and another man - that they did not consider touching the penis to require wudu."
"466","488 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us. (H). 489 - And Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from 'Imran ibn Husayn, similar."
"467","490 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Humayd al-Tawil informed us, from al-Hasan, from 'Imran ibn Husayn, the same. If following Ibn 'Umar were binding in such a matter, then following those we mentioned is worthier than following Ibn 'Umar. And that was narrated from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib and al-Hasan."
"468","490 - 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Khushaysh narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, that he did not consider touching the penis to require wudu."
"469","491 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, the same."
"470","492 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn 'Imran narrated to us, he said: Ash'ath narrated to us, from al-Hasan, that he disliked touching the private part, but if one did it, he did not consider wudu due upon him."
"471","493 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus informed us, from al-Hasan, that he did not consider touching the penis to require wudu. This we adopt, and it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"472","Chapter: wiping over the khuffs - how long for the resident and the traveler. 494 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Razin narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from 'Ubadah ibn Nusayy, from Ubayy ibn 'Umarah... (and Ubayy ibn 'Umarah prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) facing both qiblas) that he said: O Messenger of Allah, may I wipe over the khuffs? He said: Yes. He said: one day, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Yes, and two days. He said: and two days, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Yes, and three. He said: and three, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Yes, until it reached seven, then he said: wipe as long as you wish."
"473","495 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn 'Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Razin, that he informed him, from Muhammad ibn Yazid, from Ayyub ibn Qatan, from 'Ubadah, from Ubayy ibn 'Umarah, he said: (and he was among those who prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) facing both qiblas) from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar."
"474","496 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Ibn 'Ufayr narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Razin, from Muhammad ibn Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Ayyub ibn Qatan, from 'Ubadah, from Ubayy ibn 'Umarah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), similar. Some people adopted this and said: there is no time limit for wiping over the khuffs, in travel nor in residence. They said: what was narrated from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab also strengthened that... then they cited what:"
"475","497 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: Musa ibn 'Ulayy narrated to us, from his father, from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir, he said: I came in a delegation from Syria to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, leaving Syria on Friday and entering Medina on Friday. I entered upon 'Umar wearing Jurmuqaniyyah khuffs. He said to me: when did you last take off your khuffs, O 'Uqbah? I said: I put them on Friday, and this is Friday. He said to me: you have hit the Sunnah."
"476","498 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: al-Mufaddal ibn Fadalah (judge of Egypt) narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Hakam al-Balawi, from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir, the same."
"477","499 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: 'Amr, Ibn Lahi'ah, and al-Layth informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Hakam al-Balawi, that he heard 'Ulayy ibn Rabah al-Lakhmi report from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir... then he mentioned the same, except that he said: so he said: you are right, and he did not say: the Sunnah. They said: 'Umar's saying to 'Uqbah, you have hit the Sunnah, indicates that in his view that is from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), because the Sunnah can only be from him. Others opposed them in that and said: rather the resident wipes over his khuffs a day and a night, and the traveler three days and their nights. They said: as for what you narrated from 'Umar, his saying you have hit the Sunnah, there is no proof in that that in his view it is from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), because the Sunnah may be from him and may be from his caliphs. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Hold to my Sunnah and the Sunnah of the rightly-guided caliphs."
"478","500 - Abu Umayyah narrated it to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Thawr ibn Yazid, from Khalid ibn Ma'dan, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Amr al-Sulami, from al-'Irbad ibn Sariyah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib said to Rabi'ah (about the blood-money of a woman's fingers): O son of my brother, it is the Sunnah, meaning the statement of Zayd ibn Thabit. So it is possible that 'Umar considered what he said to 'Uqbah - and he is of the rightly-guided caliphs - and called his view that, Sunnah. Besides, consecutive (mutawatir) reports have come from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on this, fixing the time of wiping for the traveler and the resident, contrary to what the hadith of Ubayy ibn 'Umarah brings. Among what was narrated from him on that is what:"
"479","501 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: al-Firyabi narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Qays, from al-Hakam ibn 'Utaybah, from al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah, from Shurayh ibn Hani', from 'Ali, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fixed three days and their nights for the traveler, and a day and a night for the resident - meaning wiping over the khuffs."
"480","502 - Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah, from Shurayh ibn Hani', he said: I saw 'Ali and asked him about wiping over the khuffs, and he said: when we were traveling we were ordered to wipe three days and their nights, and when we were resident, a day and a night."
"481","503 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Talhah narrated to us, from Zubayd, from al-Hakam ibn 'Utaybah, from Shurayh ibn Hani', he said: I came to 'A'ishah and said: O Mother of the Believers, what do you think about wiping over the khuffs? She said: go to 'Ali; he knows better about that than I; he used to travel with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So I asked him, and he said: when we were traveling with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he ordered us not to remove our khuffs for three days and three nights."
"482","504 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from 'Amr ibn Maymun, from Abu 'Abdullah al-Jadali, from Khuzaymah ibn Thabit, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he fixed wiping over the khuffs for the traveler at three days and their nights, and for the resident a day and a night. He said: had the questioner pressed him further in his question, he would have granted him more."
"483","505 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Sufyan and Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur... then he mentioned the same with its isnad, except that he said: had we asked for more, he would have granted us more."
"484","506 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Bishr ibn 'Umar narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibrahim, from Abu 'Abdullah al-Jadali, from Khuzaymah ibn Thabit, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he fixed wiping over the khuffs for the traveler at three and their nights, and for the resident a day and a night. He said: had the questioner pressed him further in his question, he would have granted him more."
"485","507 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Yahya narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim... then he mentioned the same with its isnad."
"486","508 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam and Hammad, from Ibrahim... then he mentioned the same with its isnad."
"487","509 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud and Abu 'Amir narrated to us, they said: Hisham narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim... then he mentioned the same with its isnad."
"488","510 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us. (H). 511 - And Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hudbah narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Ma'shar, from Ibrahim, from Abu 'Abdullah al-Jadali, from Khuzaymah, that he witnessed that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that."
"489","512 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from Abu 'Abdullah, from Khuzaymah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"490","513 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: al-Hakam and Hammad informed us, from Ibrahim, with its isnad, the same."
"491","514 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, he said: al-Sa'q ibn Hazn narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn al-Hakam narrated to us, from al-Minhal ibn 'Amr, and from Zirr ibn Hubaysh al-Asadi, from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, he said: I was sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when a man of Murad called Safwan ibn 'Assal came and said: O Messenger of Allah, I travel between Mecca and Medina, so give me fatwa about wiping over the khuffs. He said: three days for the traveler, and a day and a night for the resident."
"492","515 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Asim, from Zirr, he said: I came to Safwan ibn 'Assal and said: wiping over the khuffs after stool and urine troubles my conscience, so did you hear anything from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that? He said: yes; when we were traveling, we were ordered not to remove our khuffs for three days and their nights except for janabah, but (we kept them) for stool and urine."
"493","516 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from 'Asim... then he mentioned the same with its isnad."
"494","517 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from 'Asim ibn Bahdalah, then he mentioned the same with its isnad."
"495","518 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Affan narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Abu Rawq 'Atiyyah ibn al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Gharif 'Ubaydullah ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from Safwan ibn 'Assal, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me on a detachment and said: for the traveler three, and for the resident a day and a night, wiping over the khuffs."
"496","519 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Abi al-Wazir narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Wahhab al-Thaqafi narrated to us, from Muhajir, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, adding: when you put them on in a state of purity."
"497","520 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Hushaym informed us, he said: Dawud ibn 'Amr al-Hadrami informed us, from Busr ibn 'Ubaydullah al-Hadrami, from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, he said: 'Awf ibn Malik al-Ashja'i narrated to us, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same regarding the timing specifically, adding that he fixed that at the expedition of Tabuk."
"498","521 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Dawud... then he mentioned the same with its isnad."
"499","522 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Yazid narrated to us, from 'Amir, from 'Urwah ibn al-Mughirah, that he heard his father say: we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he went to relieve himself; I brought him water; he was wearing a Syrian cloak; he performed wudu and wiped over the khuffs. So it became Sunnah: for the traveler three days and their nights, and for the resident a day and a night."
"500","523 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Ali ibn Rabi'ah, from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), about wiping over the khuffs: for the resident a day and a night, and for the traveler three days and their nights. These reports are consecutive (mutawatir) from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fixing the time of wiping over the khuffs at three days and their nights for the traveler and a day and a night for the resident. No one should abandon such consecutive reports for the likes of the hadith of Ubayy ibn 'Umarah. As for what they argued from what 'Uqbah narrated from 'Umar, consecutive reports have also come from 'Umar contradicting that."
"501","524 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from 'Imran ibn Muslim, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, he said: we said to Nubatah al-Ju'fi - and he was the boldest of us with 'Umar: ask him about wiping over the khuffs. So he asked him, and he said: for the traveler three days and their nights, and for the resident a day and a night."
"502","525 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, he said: 'Imran ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, that Nubatah asked 'Umar about that, and he said: wipe over them a day and a night."
"503","526 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Malik ibn Mighwal informed us, from 'Imran ibn Muslim, from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, he said: we came to 'Umar, and Nubatah asked him about wiping over the khuffs, and 'Umar said: for the traveler three days and their nights, and for the resident a day and a night."
"504","527 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from Nubatah, from 'Umar, the same."
"505","529 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Amir, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, from Hammad, then he mentioned the same with its isnad."
"506","530 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Muslim narrated to us, he said: Hisham narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from 'Umar, the same."
"507","531 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id al-Asbahani narrated to us, he said: Hafs informed us, from 'Asim, from Abu 'Uthman, that 'Umar said: whoever puts his feet in (the khuffs) while they are pure, let him wipe over them until the same hour of his day and night."
"508","532 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Zayd ibn Wahb, he said: 'Umar wrote to us about wiping over the khuffs: for the traveler three days and their nights, and for the resident a day and a night. So this is 'Umar: what agrees with what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the timing for the traveler and the resident has come from him. And the hadith of 'Uqbah may also mean that statement was from 'Umar because he knew that 'Uqbah's route by which he came was a route with no water in it, so his ruling was tayammum. So he asked him: when did you last take off your khuffs, given that your ruling is tayammum? And he informed him what he informed him. This construction is the worthiest interpretation of this hadith, so that it agrees with what was narrated from 'Umar besides it and does not contradict it. And what agrees with what we narrated about the timing was narrated from Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) other than 'Umar."
"509","533 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn Mukhaymirah, from Shurayh ibn Hani', he said: I came to 'A'ishah and asked her about wiping over the khuffs. She said: go to 'Ali; he is the most knowledgeable of them about the wudu of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he used to travel with him. So I went to him and asked him, and he said: a day and a night for the resident, and three days and their nights for the traveler."
"510","534 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from al-Harith ibn Suwayd, he said: 'Abdullah fixed wiping over the khuffs at three days for the traveler, and a day for the resident."
"511","535 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from al-Mughirah, from Ibrahim, from 'Amr ibn al-Harith, he said: I traveled with 'Abdullah, and he would not remove his khuffs for three (days)."
"512","536 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Samad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Musa ibn Salamah, he said: I asked Ibn 'Abbas about wiping over the khuffs. He said: for the traveler three days and their nights, and for the resident a day and a night."
"513","537 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, then he mentioned the same with its isnad."
"514","538 - Salih narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Ghaylan ibn 'Abdullah informed me, he said: I heard Ibn 'Umar say that."
"515","539 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hudbah narrated to us, he said: Sallam ibn Miskin narrated to us, from 'Abd al-'Aziz, from Anas, the same."
"516","540 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Qatan, from Abu Zayd al-Ansari, from a man of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same."
"517","541 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Yunus and Qatadah, from Musa ibn Salamah, from Ibn 'Abbas, the same. These are the statements of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), unanimous on the timing we mentioned for wiping over the khuffs for the traveler and the resident. No one should oppose that. And what we mentioned is also the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"518","Chapter: the junub, the menstruating woman, and one without wudu, and their reciting the Qur'an. 542 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Ata' narrated to us, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Hudayn Abu Sasan, from al-Muhajir ibn Qunfudh, that he greeted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was performing wudu, and he did not return it. When he finished his wudu he said: nothing prevented me from returning to you except that I disliked remembering Allah, Mighty and Majestic, except in purity."
"519","543 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: Humaydah and others informed us, from al-Hasan, from al-Muhajir, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was urinating - or he said: I passed by him after he had urinated - so I greeted him, and he did not return to me until he finished his wudu, then he returned to me. Some people adopted this and said: no one should remember Allah the Exalted with anything except in a state in which it is valid for him to pray. Others opposed them in that and said: one who is greeted while in a state of hadath performs tayammum and returns the salam, even in town. And they said about other than the salam the same as the first view. Among what they argued with in that is what:"
"520","544 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated it to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Thabit al-'Abdi narrated to us. (H). 545 - And Husayn ibn Nasr and Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, they said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Thabit narrated to us, he said: Nafi' narrated to us, he said: I went with Ibn 'Umar to Ibn 'Abbas on an errand of Ibn 'Umar's, and he fulfilled his errand. Part of his talk that day was that he said: a man passed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a lane, after he had come from stool or urine, and greeted him, but he did not return the salam until the man was almost hidden in the lane. Then he struck his hands on the wall, performing tayammum for his face, then struck another blow performing tayammum for his forearms. He said: then he returned the salam to him and said: nothing prevented me from returning the salam to you except that I was not pure."
"521","546 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, he said: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Dahhak ibn 'Uthman, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that a man greeted the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was urinating, and he did not return to him until he came to a wall and performed tayammum."
"522","547 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, he said: al-Layth narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Hurmuz, from 'Umayr, the freedman of Ibn 'Abbas, that he heard him say: 'Abdullah ibn Yasar, the freedman of Maymunah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I came to Abu al-Juhaym ibn al-Harith ibn al-Simmah al-Ansari. Abu al-Juhaym said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came from the direction of Bi'r Jamal; a man met him and greeted him, but the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not return to him until he faced the wall, wiped his face and hands, then returned the salam to him."
"523","548 - Abu Zur'ah 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Amr al-Dimashqi narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Muhammad al-Naqid narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, from 'Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj, from 'Umayr, the freedman of Ibn 'Abbas... then he mentioned the same. They said: by these reports we permitted one who is greeted while not pure to perform tayammum and return the salam, so that it is an answer to the salam. This is like those who permitted tayammum for the funeral prayer and the two Eids when missing them is feared if one busies himself seeking water for wudu. And they cited in that what:"
"524","549 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Hassan narrated to us, he said: 'Umar ibn Ayyub al-Mawsili narrated to us, from al-Mughirah ibn Ziyad, from 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas, about a man surprised by a funeral while without wudu, he said: he performs tayammum and prays over it."
"525","550 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn 'Awn narrated to us, he said: Hushaym informed us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim and 'Abd al-Malik, from 'Ata', and Zakariyya from 'Amir, and Yunus from al-Hasan, the same."
"526","551 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, the same."
"527","552 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Mu'ammal narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, the same."
"528","553 - Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hammad, from Ibrahim, the same."
"529","554 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, and Mughirah from Ibrahim, and 'Abd al-Malik from 'Ata', similar."
"530","555 - Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, from 'Abbad ibn Rashid, he said: I heard al-Hasan say that."
"531","556 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, the same. He said: and al-Layth said the same to me."
"532","557 - Abu Bishr al-Raqqi narrated to us, he said: Shuja' ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Ghaniyyah, from al-Hakam, the same. Since tayammum in towns was permitted fearing missing the funeral prayer and the two Eid prayers - because if missed they are not made up - they said: likewise we permitted tayammum in towns for returning the salam, so that it is an answer to the Muslim's salam, because if it is not done and he does not return the salam then, that is missed, and if he returns after that, it is not an answer to it. As for other than that, of dhikr and Qur'an recitation whose missing is not feared, no one should do that except in purity. Others opposed them in that and said: there is no harm in remembering Allah the Exalted in all states, of janabah and otherwise, and reciting the Qur'an in that, except janabah and menstruation, for their companion should not recite the Qur'an. And they argued in that with what:"
"533","558 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, from 'Abdullah ibn Salamah, he said: 'Ali and I, and a man of ours and a man of Banu Asad, went to 'Ali; he sent them off, then said: you are two stout men, so strive for your religion. He said: then he entered the privy, then came out, took a handful of water, wiped with it, and began reciting the Qur'an. Seeing as if we disapproved of that, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would come out of the privy, teach us the Qur'an, and eat meat with us; nothing barred him from that except janabah."
"534","559 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Murrah informed us, he said: I heard 'Abdullah ibn Salamah... then he mentioned the same, except that he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would relieve himself then recite the Qur'an."
"535","560 - Husayn ibn Nasr and Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, they said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us... then he mentioned the same with its isnad."
"536","561 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us... then he mentioned the same with its isnad."
"537","562 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: 'Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Murrah said, from 'Abdullah ibn Salamah, from 'Ali, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would recite the Qur'an in every state except janabah."
"538","563 - Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus al-Susi narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Isa narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from 'Amr, from 'Abdullah ibn Salamah, from 'Ali, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would teach us the Qur'an in every state except janabah. Abu Ja'far said: in what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is permission to remember Allah the Exalted without wudu, and to recite the Qur'an likewise, and barring the junub from reciting the Qur'an specifically. And there was also narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what indicates permission to remember Allah the Exalted without purity, namely what:"
"539","564 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Shimr ibn 'Atiyyah, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, he said: Abu Zabyah narrated to us, he said: I heard 'Amr ibn 'Abasah say: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: no Muslim sleeps pure remembering Allah, then wakes at night asking Allah the Exalted something of the affairs of this world and the next, but He gives it to him."
"540","565 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: 'Affan narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: 'Asim ibn Bahdalah, Thabit, and I were together, then 'Asim narrated from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from Abu Zabyah, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same, except that he did not mention his saying: remembering Allah. Thabit said: he came to us and narrated this hadith to us, and I only know he meant Abu Zabyah. I said to Hammad: from Mu'adh? He said: from Mu'adh."
"541","566 - Rabi' al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from 'Asim ibn Abi al-Nujud, from Shimr ibn 'Atiyyah... then he mentioned the same with its isnad. This is also after sleep, so in that is permission to remember Allah the Exalted after hadath. And something of that was narrated from 'A'ishah."
"542","567 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, from his father, from Khalid ibn Salamah, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah, she said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would remember Allah in all his states. So in this is permission to remember Allah, Mighty and Majestic, in janabah, but there is nothing in it - nor in the hadith of Abu Zabyah - about reciting the Qur'an. And in the hadith of 'Ali is the distinction between reciting the Qur'an and remembering Allah the Exalted in janabah. And there was also narrated about prohibiting reciting the Qur'an in janabah what:"
"543","568 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: neither the junub nor the menstruating woman recites the Qur'an."
"544","569 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us, (H). 570 - And Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us, he said: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us, they said: 'Abdullah ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Sulayman, from Tha'labah ibn Abi al-Kanud, from Malik ibn 'Ubadah al-Ghafiqi, he said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ate while junub; I informed 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, so he dragged me to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, this one informed me that you ate while junub. He said: yes; when I perform wudu I eat and drink, but I do not pray nor recite until I bathe. In these two reports is barring the junub from reciting the Qur'an, and in one of them barring the menstruating woman from that. So it is established by these two hadiths, with the hadith of 'Ali, that there is no harm in remembering Allah and reciting the Qur'an in hadath other than janabah and menstruation, and that reciting the Qur'an specifically is disliked in janabah and menstruation. So we wanted to see which of these reports is later, to make it abrogate what preceded."
"545","571 - We looked into that, and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, he said: Mu'awiyah ibn Hisham narrated to us, from Shayban, from Jabir, from 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Alqamah ibn al-Faghwa', from his father, he said: when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) urinated, we would speak to him and he would not speak to us, and greet him and he would not return to us, until: O you who believe, when you rise for prayer, was revealed. 'Alqamah reports in this hadith from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that the ruling of the junub in his view, before this verse was revealed, was not to speak and not to return the salam, until Allah, Mighty and Majestic, abrogated that with this verse, requiring purity by it only for one intending prayer. Thus it is established that the hadith of Abu al-Jahm, and the hadith of Ibn 'Umar, Ibn 'Abbas, and al-Muhajir, are all abrogated, and that the ruling in the hadith of 'Ali is later than the ruling in them."
"546","572 - And what Fahd narrated to us also indicates that, he said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, he said: al-Hasan ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: I heard Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: Ibn 'Abbas and Ibn 'Umar would recite the Qur'an while without wudu."
"547","573 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl... then he mentioned similar with its isnad."
"548","574 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Salamah. (H). 575 - And Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Humayd, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, the same."
"549","576 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Sayrafi narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, he said: Qatadah narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Buraydah, from Ibn 'Abbas, that he would recite his portion (of Qur'an) while in hadath."
"550","577 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, he said: al-Azraq ibn Qays informed me, from a man called Aban, he said: I said to Ibn 'Umar: when I urinate, may I remember Allah? He said: what do you mean by when you urinate? He said: when I pass urine. He said: yes, remember Allah. So this is Ibn 'Abbas and Ibn 'Umar: they narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he did not return the salam in hadath until performing tayammum, yet they recited the Qur'an in hadath. That is not permissible in our view unless abrogation was established with them too. And people followed them in what they held."
"551","578 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Hammad al-Kufi, from Ibrahim, that Ibn Mas'ud was teaching a man; when they reached the bank of the Euphrates the man stopped. He said to him: what is wrong with you? He said: I broke wudu. He said: recite. So he recited while he corrected him."
"552","579 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from 'Asim al-Ahwal, from 'Azrah, from Salman, that he broke wudu then recited. He was asked: do you recite though you broke wudu? He said: yes; I am not junub."
"553","580 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, he said: I asked Qatadah about a man reciting the Qur'an while not pure. He said: I heard Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib say: Abu Hurayrah would sometimes recite a surah while not pure."
"554","581 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Qatadah, from Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah, the same."
"555","582 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah... then he mentioned the same with its isnad. So it is established, by authenticating what we narrated, that the hadith of Ibn 'Abbas and those who followed him is abrogated, and that the hadith of 'Ali stands as fortified by the statements of the Companions. By that we take: we dislike a complete verse's recitation for the junub and the menstruating woman, see no harm in that for one without wudu, and see no harm for all of them in remembering Allah the Exalted. And there was narrated from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab about barring the junub also from reciting the Qur'an what agrees with what we said."
"556","583 - Ibrahim ibn Muhammad al-Sayrafi narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us, he said: Za'idah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Shaqiq, from 'Abidah, he said: 'Umar disliked reciting the Qur'an while junub."
"557","584 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: 'Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, he said: my father narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us... then he mentioned the same with its isnad. This in our view is worthier than the statement of Ibn 'Abbas, because of what agreed with it of what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, Ibn 'Umar, Abu Musa, and Malik ibn 'Ubadah. And it is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them. And there was also narrated from Ibn 'Abbas what indicates contrary to what Nafi' narrated from him in the hadith of Muhammad ibn Thabit which we cited earlier in this book of ours."
"558","585 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Sa'id ibn al-Huwayrith, from Ibn 'Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out of the privy and ate. He was asked: will you not perform wudu? He said: I do not intend to pray, so (why) should I perform wudu?"
"559","586 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn al-Huwayrith informed me... then he mentioned the same with its isnad."
"560","587 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, he said: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Dinar... then he mentioned the same with its isnad."
"561","588 - Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from 'Amr, the same with its isnad. Do you not see that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: will you not perform wudu? He said: I do not intend prayer, so (why) should I perform wudu? He informed that wudu is only intended for prayer, not for dhikr. This contradicts what we narrated from Ibn 'Abbas at the beginning of this chapter. And this is worthier, because Ibn 'Abbas acted upon it after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), showing his acting upon it to be the abrogating one. If an objector objects with what:"
"562","589 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr informed us, he said: Jabir narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from his father, from 'A'ishah, she said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never came from the privy without performing wudu like his wudu for prayer when he came out of it. They said: this shows the corruption of what you narrated from 'A'ishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would remember Allah in all his states. It was said to him: there is no proof in this for what you mentioned, because he may have performed wudu when he came out of the privy (stool) and not performed wudu when he urinated, so that time in which he would remember Allah was that time. So the meaning of her saying, he would remember Allah in all his states, is: in his purity and his hadath, so that the reports do not contradict. Besides, the hadith of Ibn 'Abbas from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) opposed that, when he said: I do not intend prayer, so (why) should I perform wudu? That shows he would only perform wudu when intending prayer. So what 'A'ishah witnessed of wudu upon his coming out may have been due to his intending prayer, not due to coming out of the privy. It is also possible that it reports what he did before the verse was revealed, and what is in the hadith of Khalid ibn Salamah reports what he did after the verse was revealed, so that what was narrated from her and from others agrees and nothing of it contradicts."
"563","Chapter: the ruling of the urine of the boy and the girl before they eat food. 590 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, he said: Bakr ibn Khalaf narrated to us, he said: Mu'adh ibn Hisham narrated to us, he said: my father informed me, from Qatadah, from Abu Harb ibn Abi al-Aswad, from his father, from 'Ali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said about the infant: the girl's urine is washed, and the boy's urine is sprinkled."
"564","591 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Qabus ibn al-Mukhariq, from Lubabah bint al-Harith: that al-Husayn ibn 'Ali urinated on the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I said: give me your garment to wash it. He said: only the female's is washed, and the male's urine is sprinkled."
"565","592 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us... then he mentioned the same with its isnad."
"566","593 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Malik, al-Layth, 'Amr, and Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Utbah, from Umm Qays bint Mihsan: that she came with a son of hers who had not eaten food to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seated him in his lap; he urinated on his garment; so he called for water, sprinkled it, and did not wash it."
"567","594 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri... then he mentioned the same with its isnad."
"568","595 - Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Raja' narrated to us, he said: Za'idah informed us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from 'A'ishah, she said: a boy was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for tahnik and supplication for him; he urinated on him; so he called for water, sprinkled it, and did not wash it. Abu Ja'far said: some people distinguished between the ruling of the boy's urine and the girl's urine before they eat food. They said: the boy's urine is pure, and the girl's urine is impure. Others opposed them in that, equating both of their urines and making them both impure. They said: the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying, the boy's urine is sprinkled, may mean by sprinkling the pouring of water over it. The Arabs call that sprinkling, such as the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: I know a city with the sea sprinkling its side - he did not mean spraying by that sprinkling, but meant adjoining its side. They said: he distinguished between them only because the boy's urine lands in one spot due to the narrowness of its outlet, while the girl's urine spreads due to the wideness of its outlet. So he ordered sprinkling for the boy's urine - meaning pouring water in one spot - and meant by washing the girl's urine tracing it with water, because it falls in scattered spots. This is plausible for what we mentioned. And there was narrated from some of the early ones what indicates that. Among that is what:"
"569","596 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, that he said: sprinkling for sprinkling, and pouring for pouring, of all urine."
"570","597 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Humayd, from al-Hasan, that he said: the girl's urine is washed, and the boy's urine is followed with water. Do you not see that Sa'id equated the ruling of all urine, of children and others? He made what of it is a spray purified by spraying, and what of it is a flow purified by pouring. Not that some of it in his view is pure and some impure, but all of it in his view is impure, with a difference in purification from its impurity in his view, by the narrowness and wideness of its outlet. Then we wanted after that to look into the reports from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): is there in them what indicates any of what we mentioned? So we looked into that."
"571","598 - Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from his father, from 'A'ishah, she said: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would be brought boys and supplicate for them; once a boy was brought who urinated on him, and he said: pour water over him, pouring."
"572","599 - Rabi' narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Khazim narrated to us... then he mentioned the same with its isnad."
"573","600 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: 'Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Hisham, from his father, from 'A'ishah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought a boy; he urinated on him; so he followed it with water and did not wash it."
"574","601 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Hisham... then he mentioned the same with its isnad, except that he did not say: and did not wash it. Following with water has the ruling of washing. Do you not see that had dung hit a man's garment and he followed it with water until it removed it, his garment is purified. And Za'idah narrated this hadith from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, saying in it: so he called for water and sprinkled it over him. And Malik, Abu Mu'awiyah, and 'Abdah narrated from Hisham ibn 'Urwah: so he called for water and poured it over him. That shows that sprinkling - in their usage - is pouring."
"575","602 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from 'Isa ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Abu Layla, he said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when al-Hasan was brought; he urinated on him; the people wanted to hurry him; so he said: my son, my son. When he finished urinating, he poured water over him."
"576","603 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Waki' informed us, from Ibn Abi Layla... then he mentioned the same with its isnad."
"577","604 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Salih narrated to us, he said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Isa, from his grandfather 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from his father, he said: I was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with Hasan or Husayn on his belly or chest; he urinated on him until I saw his urine in streams. We rose toward him, and he said: leave him. Then he called for water and poured it over him."
"578","605 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Simak, from Qabus, from Umm al-Fadl, she said: when al-Husayn was born I said: O Messenger of Allah, give him to me to care for him, or hand him to me to nurse him with my milk. So he did. I brought him to him and he placed him on his chest; he urinated on him and hit his waist-wrapper. I said to him: O Messenger of Allah, give me your wrapper to wash it. He said: only the boy's urine is poured upon, and the girl's urine is washed. Abu Ja'far said: this is Umm al-Fadl: in this hadith of hers, only the boy's urine is poured upon. And in her hadith which we cited in the first section, only the boy's urine is sprinkled. Since what we cited is so, it is established that the sprinkling intended in the first hadith is the pouring mentioned here, so that the two reports do not contradict. And this is Abu Layla: it was unanimously reported from him that he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pour water on the urine. So it is established by these reports that the ruling of the boy's urine is washing, except that pouring suffices as that washing, and that the ruling of the girl's urine is washing too. He distinguished in wording between them though they are equal in meaning, for the reason we mentioned, of the narrowness and wideness of the outlet. This is the ruling of this chapter by way of reports. As for its aspect by way of reasoning, we see the boy and the girl: the ruling of their urines is the same after they eat food. By reasoning, it should also be the same before they eat food. So if the girl's urine is impure, the boy's urine is impure too. And this is the view of Abu Hanifah, Abu Yusuf, and Muhammad, may Allah the Exalted have mercy on them."
"579","Chapter: a man who finds only date-nabidh - may he perform wudu with it, or perform tayammum? 606 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, he said: Qays ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, from Hanash al-San'ani, from Ibn 'Abbas: that Ibn Mas'ud went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the night of the jinn. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked him: with you, Ibn Mas'ud, any water? He said: with me is nabidh in my skin. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: pour for me. So he performed wudu with it and said: a drink and a purifier."
"580","607 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu 'Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, he said: 'Ali ibn Zayd ibn Jud'an informed me, from Abu Rafi', the freedman of Ibn 'Umar's daughter, from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, that he was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the night of the jinn, and that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) needed water to perform wudu with, and there was only nabidh with him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: a wholesome date and pure water. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed wudu with it. Abu Ja'far said: some people held that whoever finds only date-nabidh on his journey performs wudu with it, and they argued with these reports. Among those who held that is Abu Hanifah. Others opposed them in that and said: wudu is not performed with date-nabidh; whoever finds nothing else performs tayammum and does not perform wudu with it. Among those who held this view is Abu Yusuf. Part of the proof for the people of this view against the first view is that what was cited from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud at the beginning of this chapter was only narrated through the chains we described, and these chains are not chains by which proof stands with one who accepts solitary reports, nor did it come with manifest transmission, as required of one who acts on a report when narrations about it are consecutive. So this is of what need not be acted upon, for what we mentioned, on the doctrine of both parties we mentioned. And there was narrated from Abu 'Ubaydah ibn 'Abdullah what indicates that 'Abdullah was not with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that night."
"581","608 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, he said: Ghundar narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, he said: I said to Abu 'Ubaydah: (Was 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the night of the jinn? He said: No)."
"582","609 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb narrated to us, from Shu'bah... and he mentioned the like of it with its chain. When Abu 'Ubaydah denied that his father was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that night, and such a matter would not be hidden from one like him, thereby what others reported - that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did on that night while he was with him - is invalidated. If one says: the first reports are worthier because they are connected while this is disconnected, since Abu 'Ubaydah heard nothing from his father; it is said to him: we did not use Abu 'Ubaydah's statement as proof from that angle; we used it because one like him, with his precedence in knowledge, his standing with 'Abdullah, and his close contact with his inner circle after him, would not be unaware of such a matter of his. So we made his statement a proof in what we mentioned, not by the route it was set upon. And we have reported from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, by a connected chain, words that agreed with what Abu 'Ubaydah said."
"583","610 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: 'Amr ibn 'Awn narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abdullah narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Abu Ma'shar, from Ibrahim, from 'Alqamah, from 'Abdullah, he said: I was not with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the night of the jinn, and I would have loved to have been with him."
"584","611 - Rabi' al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, from 'Amir, from 'Alqamah, from Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him): (Was anyone with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the night of the jinn?) He said: None of us accompanied him, but we missed him one night and said: he was spirited away or assassinated, so we scattered in the ravines and valleys seeking him, and we spent the worst night a people ever spent, saying: was he spirited away or assassinated. He said: A caller of the jinn came to me, so I went to recite the Qur'an to them, and he showed us their traces. So this is 'Abdullah denying that he was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the night of the jinn. If this chapter is taken by soundness of chain, then this hadith containing the denial is worthier, due to the uprightness of its route and text and the firmness of its narrators. And if by reasoning, we have seen the agreed-upon principle that ablution is not made with raisin nabidh nor with vinegar, so by analogy date nabidh is likewise. And the scholars agreed that date nabidh, when water is present, is not used for ablution because it is not water; since it is outside the ruling of waters when water exists, so it is in the absence of water. And the hadith of Ibn Mas'ud about performing ablution with date nabidh only says the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed ablution with it while not traveling, for he had left Makkah heading to them, and it is said he performed ablution with date nabidh in that place, which has the ruling of one in Makkah since he prays full prayer, so his use of that nabidh there has the ruling of using it in Makkah, where ablution with it is not permitted by agreement. So this too is invalidated."
"585","20 - Chapter on wiping over sandals. 612 - Abu Bakrah and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu Dawud narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us. Ha."
"586","613 - And Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, he said: Hammad narrated to us, from Ya'la ibn 'Ata', from Aws ibn Abi Aws, he said: I saw my father perform ablution and wipe over a pair of sandals of his. I said to him: Do you wipe over sandals? He said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiping over sandals."
"587","614 - Fahd narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik reported to us, from Ya'la ibn 'Ata', from Aws ibn Abi Aws, he said: I was with my father on a journey and we stopped at a Bedouin water, so he urinated and performed ablution, and wiped over his sandals. I said to him: Do you do this? He said: I do not add for you beyond what I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) do. Abu Ja'far said: So a group went to wiping over sandals just as over leather socks, and they said: what was reported from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) strengthens that. They mentioned in that what..."
"588","615 - Abu Bakrah narrated to us, he said: Abu Dawud and Wahb narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Abu Zibyan, that he saw 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) urinate standing, then call for water and perform ablution, and wipe over his sandals, then enter the mosque, take off his sandals, then pray. Others opposed them in that and said: We do not hold wiping over sandals. Part of their proof is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) may have wiped over sandals beneath which were socks, intending his wiping for his socks not his sandals; and his socks, had he worn them without sandals, he could have wiped over them, so his wiping was a wiping meant for the socks, covering both socks and sandals; the wiping over the socks is what purified him, and over the sandals was extra. And what clarifies that is what..."
"589","616 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, he said: al-Mu'alla ibn Mansur narrated to us, he said: 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Abu Sinan, from al-Dahhak ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Musa: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped over his socks and his sandals."
"590","617 - Abu Bakrah and Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, they said: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Abu Qays, from Hudhayl ibn Shurahbil, from al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. So Abu Musa and al-Mughirah reported how the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiping over his sandals was. And another view was reported from Ibn 'Umar in that."
"591","618 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: Ahmad ibn al-Husayn al-Luhbi narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from Nafi': that Ibn 'Umar, when he performed ablution with his sandals on his feet, would wipe over the tops of his feet with his hands and say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do like this. So Ibn 'Umar reported that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at times when he wiped over his sandals wiped over his feet. It is possible that what he wiped over his feet was the obligation, and what he wiped over his sandals was extra. So the hadith of Abu Aws, in our view, of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiping over his sandals, may be as Abu Musa and al-Mughirah said, or as Ibn 'Umar said. If as Abu Musa and al-Mughirah said, we hold by that, for we see no harm in wiping over socks if they are thick; Abu Yusuf and Muhammad said that. As for Abu Hanifah (may Allah have mercy on him), he did not hold that unless they are thick and soled, like leather socks. And if as Ibn 'Umar said, then in that is affirmation of wiping over the feet, which is established, and what opposed and abrogated it is in the chapter on the obligation regarding the feet. On either meaning of the hadith of Aws ibn Abi Aws, from the meaning of Abu Musa and al-Mughirah, or from Ibn 'Umar, there is nothing proving permissibility of wiping over sandals. Since the hadith of Aws bears what we mentioned, with no proof for wiping over sandals, we sought that by reasoning to know its ruling. We saw the leather socks over which wiping is permitted, if torn..."
"592","21 - Chapter on how a woman with prolonged bleeding purifies herself for prayer. 619 - Muhammad ibn al-Nu'man al-Saqati narrated to us, he said: al-Humaydi narrated to us, he said: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Abi Hazim narrated to us, he said: Ibn al-Hadi narrated to me, from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm, from 'Amrah, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that Umm Habibah (may Allah be pleased with her) bint Jahsh was under 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Awf, and she suffered prolonged bleeding until she was never pure, so her case was mentioned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: It is not menstruation, but a kick from the womb; let her consider the length of her cycles in which she menstruates, leave prayer, then look at what is after that, and perform ghusl for every prayer and pray."
"593","620 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, he said: al-Wahbi narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that Umm Habibah (may Allah be pleased with her) bint Jahsh suffered prolonged bleeding in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered her to perform ghusl for every prayer. She would immerse herself in the tub filled with water, then come out of it while blood overcame it, then pray. Abu Ja'far said: So a group held that a woman with prolonged bleeding leaves prayer during her cycle days, then performs ghusl for every prayer. They argued with the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) statement in these reports, and with Umm Habibah's (may Allah be pleased with her) practice in his time."
"594","621 - al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: al-Haytham ibn Humayd narrated to us, he said: al-Nu'man, al-Awza'i, and Abu Ma'bad Hafs ibn Ghaylan reported to me, from al-Zuhri, he said: 'Urwah and 'Amrah reported to me, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: Umm Habibah (may Allah be pleased with her) bint Jahsh suffered prolonged bleeding and sought a ruling from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: This is not menstruation, but a vein ruptured by Satan; when the menstruation ends, perform ghusl and pray, and when it comes, leave prayer for it. 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: Umm Habibah (may Allah be pleased with her) would perform ghusl for every prayer, and would sometimes bathe in a tub in the room of her sister Zaynab, who was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), until the redness of blood rose over the water, then she prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and that did not prevent her from prayer."
"595","622 - Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Mu'adhdhin narrated to us, he said: Asad narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah and 'Amrah, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that Umm Habibah (may Allah be pleased with her) bint Jahsh suffered prolonged bleeding for seven years, and asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, so he ordered her to perform ghusl and said: This is a vein, not menstruation; so she would perform ghusl for every prayer."
"596","623 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, he said: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Urwah, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) the like of it. Al-Layth said: Ibn Shihab did not mention that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Umm Habibah (may Allah be pleased with her) to perform ghusl for every prayer."
"597","624 - Isma'il ibn Yahya al-Muzani narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Idris narrated to us, he said: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd reported to us: Ibn Shihab heard from 'Amrah bint 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) the like of it."
"598","625 - Isma'il narrated to us, he said: Muhammad narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from 'Amrah, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) the like of it, and he did not mention al-Layth's remark. They said: This is Umm Habibah (may Allah be pleased with her) who used to do this in the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) time, by his order to perform ghusl, so in her view it was ghusl for every prayer. And 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) and Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) said that after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and ruled by it."
"599","626 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, he said: Hammam narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Hassan, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr: that a woman came to Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him) with a letter after his sight was gone, so he handed it to his son who stammered in it, then handed it to me and I read it; he said to his son: Why did you not rattle it off as the Egyptian youth rattled it? And in it was: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, from a Muslim woman, that she suffered prolonged bleeding, and sought a ruling from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), so he ordered her to perform ghusl and pray. He said: O Allah, I know no view except what 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said, three times. Qatadah said, and 'Azrah reported to me, from Sa'id, that it was said to him: Kufa is a cold land, and ghusl for every prayer is hard on her, so he said: Had Allah willed, He would have tried her with what is harder than it."
"600","627 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, he said: al-Khasib narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr: that a woman of Kufa suffered prolonged bleeding, so she wrote to 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas, and 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, adjuring them by Allah and saying: I am a Muslim woman afflicted with a trial; I have suffered prolonged bleeding for two years, so what do you see in that? The first in whose hand the letter fell was Ibn al-Zubayr, so he said: I know for her only to leave her cycles, and perform ghusl for every prayer and pray; and they followed upon that."
